\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Tanigaki says Japan  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  stance unchanged.  \b0\par\par\uc2 288 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2005\par 02:36\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  TOKYO, March 15 (Reuters) - Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki said on Tuesday that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's basic stance on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  remained unchanged, and authorities were ready to take needed steps against excessive moves in exchange rates.   \par \par \uc2  Asked about the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market a year after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's last known \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he referred to volatility caused by geopolitical risks at that time and said: "Right now, the market seems to be reacting less to one specific factor than at that time."   \par \par \uc2  On Tokyo's stance on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  going forward, Tanigaki told a news conference: "Our basic stance is unchanged. It's desirable for \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to reflect fundamentals in a stable manner. When there is excessive volatility or speculative movements, we will take necessary measures in a timely way."   \par \par \uc2  Tokyo spent a record 20 trillion yen ($190.7 billion) in yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2003, and another 15 trillion yen in January-March 2004. It was last detected in the market on March 16, 2004.   \par \par \uc2  Asked about monetary policy as the Bank of Japan kicks off a two-day policy meeting on Tuesday, Tanigaki reiterated that he wanted the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to continue taking effective steps to beat deflation.   \par \par \uc2  He added that recent economic data showed that deflation had not yet ended.   \par \par \uc2  He also said his ministry would keep closely monitoring interest rate movements.   \par \par \uc2  On a report in the Nihon Keizai newspaper that Japan had agreed with China to end Tokyo's programme of providing fresh yen loans to Beijing at the end of fiscal 2007/08, Tanigaki said the government was finalising its plan for yen loans to China for this year and no decision had been made. ($1=104.88 yen).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050315e13f0015q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
