\par\par \b \uc2 Tanigaki:To Act Against Excessive, Speculative  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Moves  \b0\par\par\uc2 544 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2005\par 01:30\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japanese Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki said Tuesday that there is no change to the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's stance of taking action against excessive and speculative moves in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  exchange market.  \par \par \uc2 "It's desirable for \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates to move in a stable manner and to reflect economic fundamentals," Tanigaki said in a press conference.  \par \par \uc2 "We will take necessary steps in a timely manner against excessive and speculative moves" in the current \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  exchange market, Tanigaki said. [ 15-03-05 0040GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Tanigaki made the remarks when asked to comment on the one-year anniversary of Tokyo's withdrawal from \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  hasn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets since ending a 15-month, Y35 trillion \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaign on March 16, 2004.  \par \par \uc2 He said that the market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was warranted until the beginning of last year because the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market was reacting excessively to uncertainty over geopolitical risks.  \par \par \uc2 "Compared to those times, it seems the market is not responding as excessively to a single factor," Tanigaki said, explaining why the ministry hasn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market for the past year.  \par \par \uc2 Tanigaki declined to comment on speculation that the Bank of Japan may examine lowering the current account deposit target from the present range of Y30 trillion to Y35 trillion, a move that may be interpreted in the financial market as monetary tightening.  \par \par \uc2 But he said it's "necessary" for the BOJ to take "effective steps" toward ending deflation with recent economic data showing the nation hasn't overcome deflation just yet.  \par \par \uc2 Any tightening of monetary policy in the future will likely affect interest rate movements, which the ministry "hopes to continue monitoring closely," Tanigaki said.  \par \par \uc2 The BOJ is expected to keep its account balance target intact in its two-day policy-setting meeting that ends Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 Tanigaki also said the Japanese government hasn't reached a formal decision on whether or not to end new yen loans to China.  \par \par \uc2 "We will discuss the issue closely with Beijing with an eye on achieving a soft-landing in the Chinese economy," Tanigaki said.  \par \par \uc2 Tanigaki's remarks came after the Nihon Keizai Shimbun reported in its Tuesday morning edition that Japan has reached a basic agreement with China to end new yen loans in fiscal 2008.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Leika Kihara, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; leika.kihara@dowjones.com \par    -Edited by Shawn Schroter and Hugh Lawson \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 15-03-05 0148GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japan Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki said Friday that the nation must closely watch oil prices but that the price spike shouldn't have a major impact on Japan because of the economy's energy efficiency.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Going over ($57 a barrel) is serious," he told regular press conference. "We need to watch (oil prices) very closely."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 He added the government needed to watch for a possible indirect impact through higher material costs.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Benchmark light, sweet crude futures for April delivery on the New York Mercantile Exchange rallied overnight as high as $57.60 a barrel, a new record for the 22-year-old contract.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By James Simms, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; james.simms@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Shawn Schroter and Bennett Richardson [ 18-03-05 0026GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020050315e13f0000o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
