\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar recovers after Friday sell-off.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Nick Olivari  \par \uc2 702 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2005\par 18:41\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, March 14 (Reuters) - The dollar jumped 1 percent against the yen and gained against most major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday as investors bought the greenback on the view that Friday's sell-off on poor U.S. trade data may have been overdone.  \par \par \uc2 Ahead of a slew of data this week, dollar buyers are also betting that the U.S. economic recovery remains on track and that the huge current account deficit will continue to be financed by foreign central banks and other investors.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. Treasury is due to release international capital flows data for January on Tuesday, and analysts' rough estimates are for net inflows to U.S. assets of between $55 billion and $75 billion.  \par \par \uc2 Current account data is expected on Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 "There was quite a sell-off on Friday and it may have been overblown," said Michael Woolfolk, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank of New York. "U.S. data suggests the economy continues to respond well and TIC data Tuesday will probably show the current account is being financed."  \par \par \uc2 Data on Friday showed the U.S. trade deficit widened in January to $58.3 billion, the second-highest monthly total on record. That caused investors to scramble to sell dollars ahead of the weekend.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Around noon in New York on Monday the dollar was up 1 percent from\par Friday against the yen  at 105.04 yen. The euro        fell 0.8 percent\par to $1.3353.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro extended early declines after European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  President Jean Claude Trichet said in testimony to the European Parliament that he saw little inflationary pressure.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sterling fell 0.7 percent to $1.9114. Bank of England Deputy Governor Rachel Lomax spooked investors overnight, saying the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  kept rates on hold in February as it saw downside risks to its forecasts.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yen hit its weakest level against the euro since December at 140.73 yen overnight, then traded back up at around 140.27 yen in noon New York trade.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Europeans don't like this level of the cross and they want it back down," a trader said. Investors are watching to see if there is \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to weaken the euro against the yen, he said.  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  DISAPPOINTMENT  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The market shrugged off upward revisions to Japanese growth data, saying they were not as good as they appeared, and focused on the country's narrowing current account surplus.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese gross domestic product for the last quarter of 2004 was revised up to show growth of 0.1 percent from an initial 0.1 percent contraction.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The main contributor to the upward revision was a rise in inventories, which could signal weak demand. A downgrading in the GDP deflator to minus 0.4 percent on the year showed Japan's battle against deflation was not yet over.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The data doesn't offer a large degree of optimism as the deflator was revised downward," said Naomi Fink, senior \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at BNP Paribas. "The current account surplus also was smaller than expected."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Selling by Japanese investors and importers against the dollar and the euro added to the yen's losses, as did selling by option players and speculators wanting to lock in profits.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Ten-year U.S. Treasury yields , hovering near their highest levels in seven months, may also be giving the dollar a boost, analysts said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The spread between "the U.S. and Europe maybe makes you think you have to own dollars," said Michael McGuinness, head of North American sales at American Express Bank in New York.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar was also buoyed by Friday's news that net dollar short positions declined in the week to March 8, according to the Commodity Futures Trading Commission, though some traders noted the level of dollar shorts remained high.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "There's some nervousness around the IMM data. For the week, I still see the dollar weaker," said Daragh Maher, senior \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at Calyon in London.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The market had limited reaction to a statement by Chinese Premier Wen Jiabao that Beijing could spring a surprise on markets in deciding when and how it reforms its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Additional reporting by Sabyasachi Mitra in London).   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050314e13e00drb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
