\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar rebounds after Friday selloff.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Nick Olivari  \par \uc2 667 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2005\par 16:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Recasts, updates prices, adds comment, changes byline, changes dateline, previous LONDON)  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, March 14 (Reuters) - The dollar jumped against the yen and gained against other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday as investors bought the greenback on the view that Friday's sell-off on poor U.S. trade data may have been overdone.  \par \par \uc2 Ahead of a slew of economic data this week, dollar buyers are also betting that the U.S. economic recovery remains on track and that the huge current account deficit will continue to be financed by foreign central banks and other investors.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. Treasury is due to release international capital flows data on Tuesday while current account data are due on Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 "There was quite a sell-off on Friday and it may have been overblown," said Michael Woolfolk, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank of New York. "U.S. data suggests the economy continues to respond well and TIC data Tuesday will probably show the current account is being financed."  \par \par \uc2 Data on Friday showed the U.S. trade deficit widened in January to $58.3 billion, its second-highest monthly total on record. That caused investors to scramble to sell dollars ahead of the weekend.  \par \par \uc2 Mid morning in New York on Monday the dollar was up 0.7 percent against the yen at 104.74 and gained 0.5 percent versus the euro at $1.3380.  \par \par \uc2 The euro extended declines after European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Jean Claude Trichet said in testimony to the European Parliament that he sees little inflationary pressure.  \par \par \uc2 Elsewhere the yen hit its lowest level against the euro since December at 140.73 overnight, then traded back up at around 140.20 yen in morning New York trade.  \par \par \uc2 "Europeans don't like this level of the cross and they want it back down," a trader said. Investors are watching to see if there is \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the euro against the yen, he said.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 JAPAN\b0 \highlight\uc2  DISAPPOINTMENT  \par \par \uc2 The market shrugged off an upward revision to Japanese growth data, saying they were not as good as they appeared, and focused on the country's narrowing current account surplus.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese gross domestic product for the last quarter of 2004 was revised up to show growth of 0.1 percent from an initial 0.1 percent contraction.  \par \par \uc2 The main contributor to the upward revision was a rise in inventories, which could signal weak demand, and a downgrading in the GDP deflator to minus 0.4 percent on the year showed Japan's battle against deflation was not yet over.  \par \par \uc2 "The data doesn't offer a large degree of optimism as the deflator was revised downwards," said Naomi Fink, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at BNP Paribas. "The current account surplus also was smaller than expected."  \par \par \uc2 Selling by Japanese investors and importers against the dollar and the euro added to the yen's losses, as did selling by option players and speculators wanting to lock in profits.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was also buoyed on Monday by Friday's news that net dollar short positions declined in the week to March 8, according to the Commodity Futures Trading Commission, though some traders noted the level of dollar shorts remained high.  \par \par \uc2 "There's some nervousness around the IMM data. For the week, I still see the dollar weaker," said Daragh Maher, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Calyon in London.  \par \par \uc2 UBS in a research note said it remained bearish on the dollar and suggested the greenback is unlikely to find solace in rising bond yields.  \par \par \uc2 Ten-year U.S. Treasury yields are hovering near their highest yields in seven months.  \par \par \uc2 "We remain bearish on the dollar and target euro/dollar at 1.36 over three months and dollar/yen at 103," analysts at UBS said.  \par \par \uc2 The market showed limited reaction to a statement by Chinese Premier Wen Jiabao that Beijing could spring a surprise on markets in deciding when and how it reforms its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . (Additional reporting by Sabyasachi Mitra in London).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050314e13e00ci3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
