\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Japan taking more active approach on  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  reserves.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Yoko Nishikawa  \par \uc2 827 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2005\par 11:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  TOKYO, March 11 (Reuters) - Japan has been diversifying the types of dollar-denominated assets in its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, the world's largest, and has shifted to a more active management approach from its traditional buy-and-hold policy.   \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF), which controls the $840.6 billion reserves, stresses, however, that it has no plan to divert the reserves out of dollars into euros and that U.S. Treasury securities remain the staple investment vehicle.   \par \par \uc2  It also says a top priority is to avoid distorting the market, given the sheer size of the reserves, the world's largest.   \par \par \uc2  "We are becoming more efficient in managing our portfolio by ensuring the stability, liquidity and profitability of assets in each \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Kiyoto Ido, director-general of the MOF's international bureau, told Reuters.   \par \par \uc2  "For example, in the case of dollar-denominated assets, U.S. Treasuries remain the most appropriate investment targets from the stability and liquidity point of view," he said.   \par \par \uc2  "But to avoid an excessive accumulation in U.S. Treasuries, we have been diversifying the types of assets by paying attention to their profitability as well."   \par \par \uc2  Ministry officials also say they have been buying and selling securities in the secondary market, using private brokerages, rather than just buying new issues and holding them to maturity.   \par \par \uc2  "By participating in auctions only, we cannot choose the timing of our purchases. But in the case of the secondary market, we can buy at an appropriate time by looking at interest rate movements in the market," Ido said.   \par \par \uc2  LARGE INFLUENCE   \par \par \uc2  Japan's moves come at a highly sensitive time for the market, which is on the lookout for any sign of changes in reserve policies of Japan, China, Taiwan or South Korea.   \par \par \uc2  They are the world's top holders of external reserves with a combined total of nearly $2 trillion after buying dollars in the market through \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem an export-crimping rise in their own \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2  Japan's Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi rattled \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets on Thursday when he said diversification of external reserves was "necessary", stoking fears that Asian nations would cut their giant dollar reserves.   \par \par \uc2  But the MOF quickly clarified that Japan had no plan to switch its dollar reserves to euros as that would precipitate a further fall in the dollar, which is not in Japan's interests.   \par \par \uc2  "Our intention is not to distort the market with our reserves management," a second senior ministry official told Reuters.   \par \par \uc2  Japan does not give a breakdown of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in its foreign reserves, but its historical data on \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has been mostly dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , shows that most of the hefty reserves are in dollars.   \par \par \uc2  Japan's reserves ballooned on the back of a record 20 trillion yen ($192.2 billion) in yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2003 and a further 15 trillion yen in the first three months of 2004. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market since mid-March last year.   \par \par \uc2  Some lawmakers have criticised the MOF's handling of the reserves, which are set to notch unrealised losses of around 11.4 trillion yen by March 2006, when calculated in yen terms.   \par \par \uc2  The MOF argues that there will be no losses as long as the reserves are left in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as resources for future \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the event the yen falls in an undesirable way.   \par \par \uc2  "Foreign reserves exist for various purposes such as \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and there is a meaning to keep holding them as they are," Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki told parliament on Friday when asked about the reserves buildup.   \par \par \uc2  In addition, the MOF says the exchange rate losses are cushioned by interest-rate differentials between the yen and other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2  Three-month finance bills, the MOF's main funding tool for yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  funds, yields about 0.004 percent, while three-month U.S. Treasury bills yield 2.68 percent.   \par \par \uc2  MOF officials stress that making money is not their ultimate goal and that diversification does not mean Japan is ready to buy risky and illiquid assets, such as land and stocks, as some other Asian countries such as Singapore do.   \par \par \uc2  South Korea is also set to establish the Korea Investment Corp. (KIC), which will be allowed to invest in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  assets abroad and is modelled on the Government of Singapore Investment Corp. Pte Ltd. (GIC).   \par \par \uc2  Hiroshi Watanabe, Japan's top financial diplomat, said he did not need to follow suit in the foreseeable future.   \par \par \uc2  "Interest rate differentials will not reverse in my lifetime. I don't think we will need to create such an agency," Watanabe told reporters on Thursday.   \par \par \uc2  "We will be flexible and efficient in choosing what kind of dollar and euro assets we buy, but it will be far from buying Shiodome Building (a new, large office building in central Tokyo) as the GIC has done." ($1=104.06 Yen).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050311e13b006yx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
