\par\par \b \uc2 Japan steps up  \highlight22\uc2 fx \highlight\uc2  reserves management, but quietly.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Yoko Nishikawa  \par \uc2 848 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2005\par 08:37\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, March 11 (Reuters) - Japan is taking a more aggressive approach to managing its foreign reserves, the world's largest, by diversifying the types of dollar-denominated assets it holds, although "conservative and transparent" remains its mantra.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF), which controls the $840.6 billion in external reserves, stresses, however, that it has no plan to divert the reserves out of dollars into euros.  \par \par \uc2 "We are becoming more efficient in managing our portfolio by ensuring the stability, liquidity and profitability of assets in each \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Kiyoto Ido, director-general of the MOF's international bureau, told Reuters.  \par \par \uc2 "For example, in the case of dollar-denominated assets, U.S. Treasuries remain the most appropriate investment targets from the stability and liquidity point of view," he said.  \par \par \uc2 "But to avoid an excessive accumulation in U.S. Treasuries, we have been diversifying the types of assets by paying attention to their profitability as well."  \par \par \uc2 Ministry officials say the MOF has been buying and selling securities in the secondary market by using private brokerages, rather than depending solely on placing bids at auctions.  \par \par \uc2 "By participating in auctions only, we cannot choose the timing of our purchases. But in the case of the secondary market, we can buy at an appropriate time by looking at interest rate movements in the market," Ido said.  \par \par \uc2 Traditionally, the MOF holds securities bought through auctions until they mature, but it now believes that it needs to be more effective in rebalancing its reserves portfolio and trading in the secondary market is one way to do that.  \par \par \uc2 The moves come at a highly sensitive time for the market, which is on the lookout for any sign of changes in policy regarding reserves by Japan, China, Taiwan or South Korea.  \par \par \uc2 They are the world's top holders of external reserves with a combined total of nearly $2 trillion after they bought dollars in the market through \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem an export-crimping rise in their own \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi rattled \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets on Thursday when he said diversification of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves was "necessary", reviving fears that Asian nations would cut their giant dollar reserves.  \par \par \uc2 But the MOF quickly clarified that Japan had no plan to switch its dollar reserves to euros as that would precipitate a further fall in the dollar, which is not in Japan's interest.  \par \par \uc2 Japan does not give a breakdown of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in its foreign reserves, but its historical data on \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has been mostly dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , show that most of the hefty reserves are in dollars.  \par \par \uc2 "Our intention is not to distort the market with our reserves management," another senior ministry official told Reuters.  \par \par \uc2 "But foreign reserves are sources of our \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and they are part of our national assets, so we need to be efficient in managing them," he added.  \par \par \uc2 NO RISKY ASSETS  \par \par \uc2 Japan's reserves ballooned on the back of a record 20 trillion yen ($192.2 billion) in yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2003 and a further 15 trillion yen in the first three months of 2004. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market since mid-March last year.  \par \par \uc2 Some lawmakers have criticised the MOF's handling of the reserves, which are set to notch unrealised losses of around 11.4 trillion yen by March 2006, when calculated in yen terms.  \par \par \uc2 The MOF, however, insists there will be no such losses as long as the reserves are held in foreign-denominated assets as sources for future \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Interest-rate differentials between the yen and other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  offset, or at least dampen, the exchange rate losses, the MOF says.  \par \par \uc2 Ministry officials, who stress their ultimate aim is not to make profits, say that diversifying the types of assets does not mean Japan is ready to buy risky and illiquid assets, such as land and stocks, as do other Asian countries including Singapore.  \par \par \uc2 South Korea is also set to establish the Korea Investment Corporation (KIC), which will be allowed to invest in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  assets abroad and is modelled on the Government of Singapore Investment Corp. Pte Ltd. (GIC).  \par \par \uc2 Hiroshi Watanabe, Japan's top financial diplomat, said that while South Korea has decided to set up an agency to manage part of its external reserves to seek more aggressive investment returns, Japan would not follow suit in the foreseeable future.  \par \par \uc2 "Interest rate differentials (between the yen and other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) will not reverse in my lifetime. I don't think we will need to create such an agency," Watanabe said on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 "We will be flexible and efficient in choosing what kind of dollar and euro assets we buy, but it will be far from buying Shiodome Building as the GIC has done," Watanabe told reporters.  \par \par \uc2 The real estate investment arm of GIC - a global investment company that manages Singapore's foreign reserves by investing in real estate, equities and fixed income, recently bought a sprawling shopping and business office site in central Tokyo called Shiodome City Center. ($1=104.06 Yen).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050311e13b0050n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
