\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Japan MOF's Watanabe - no plan to shift  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  reserves.  \b0\par\par\uc2 336 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2005\par 12:15\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, March 10 (Reuters) - Japan's top financial diplomat said on Thursday the government had no plans for now to shift the composition of its foreign reserves and reiterated that it was ready to take action in the market if needed.  \par \par \uc2 Earlier, Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi said reserves should be diversified, triggering a decline in the dollar, although it quickly retraced losses after the Finance Ministry clarified it had no plan to shift funds out of the U.S \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "It would be unwise to sell dollars now under current market conditions," Hiroshi Watanabe, vice finance minister for international affairs, told reporters.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market has been particularly sensitive to any signs of a change in reserves after a South Korean \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  report that referred to reserve diversification sparked a fall in the dollar last month.  \par \par \uc2 Watanabe said he saw little sign of central banks reshuffling their existing reserves out of dollars.  \par \par \uc2 "If they were to sell their existing (dollar reserves) there may be quite an impact, but there has been no evidence of that in the past or recently," he said.  \par \par \uc2 "Japan has no plans to do so, either," he said, adding that the government's stance on managing its reserves was "conservative".  \par \par \uc2 Japan's reserves have ballooned to record highs after yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of a record 20 trillion yen ($192.3 billion) in 2003 and a further 15 trillion yen in the first three months of 2004.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market since mid-March last year, but Watanabe reiterated that the government was still ready to do so if needed.  \par \par \uc2 "It doesn't matter that it's been a year (since the last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 )," he said.  \par \par \uc2 "We could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  tomorrow or we could not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the next three years," he said.  \par \par \uc2 But he also said he did not see the dollar falling further against the yen under current global economic conditions. ($1=103.98 Yen).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050310e13a008z1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
