\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 4-Japan PM remarks trigger speculation on  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  reserves.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Yoko Nishikawa  \par \uc2 786 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2005\par 12:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, March 10 (Reuters) - Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi rekindled \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market speculation that Japan could shift its \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves - the world's largest - out of U.S. dollars when he said on Thursday that reserves should be diversified.  \par \par \uc2 Although the dollar quickly retraced losses after a \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) clarification that Tokyo had no plans to shift funds out of the dollar, the episode highlighted the market's wariness over Japan's policy on its $840.6 billion reserves.  \par \par \uc2 The market has been sensitive to any signs of a reserve policy change by Japan, China, Taiwan or South Korea - now the world's top holders of external reserves with combined holdings of nearly $2 trillion resulting from dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem an export-crimping rise in their own \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The latest gyration came on the heels of a similar spike down in the dollar last month after a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  report in South Korea, which has the world's fourth-largest reserves, that referred to reserves diversification.  \par \par \uc2 "I believe diversification is necessary," Koizumi told a parliamentary committee when asked about the risks of having reserves too concentrated in one \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 However, he added that Japan must consider what is in its best interests, along with safety and other issues, in making any decision on the issue.  \par \par \uc2 The MOF immediately said it had no intention to shift its funds out of the dollar, and the ministry's top \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy maker later made clear Japan did not think selling dollars was in Japan's interest.  \par \par \uc2 "It would be unwise to sell dollars now under current market conditions," Hiroshi Watanabe, vice finance minister for international affairs, told reporters.  \par \par \uc2 "We are not at a stage where we need to sell dollars in the market and immediately shift to other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he added.  \par \par \uc2 NO ROOM TO MOVE  \par \par \uc2 The dollar briefly fell about a quarter percent toward 103.70 yen and beyond $1.34 to the euro on Koizumi's remarks. It then erased losses on the MOF's initial remarks denying any plan to shuffle the reserve assets.  \par \par \uc2 "We have no plan to change the composition of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  holdings in the foreign reserves and we are not thinking about switching dollar reserves to euro holdings," a senior MOF official told Reuters.  \par \par \uc2 "They may talk about diversification, but the reserves are so huge they can't do anything about it for now," said Junya Tanase, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at JP Morgan Chase in Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 "The most plausible interpretation seems to be that they may somehow diversify in due course but Koizumi's remarks do not represent any policy change."  \par \par \uc2 U.S. Treasury securities also came under pressure after Koizumi's comments. The yield on 10-year Treasury notes rose to 4.55 percent, compared with 4.53 percent in late New York trade.  \par \par \uc2 Randal Quarles, the U.S. Treasury's assistant secretary for international affairs, said in Tokyo that moves by central banks to shift reserves composition were inevitable but that they were unlikely to harm the government's ability to finance its debt.  \par \par \uc2 Japan does not give a breakdown of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in its foreign reserves, although its quarterly reports on \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  show that virtually all of the hefty reserve increases in the last few years were in dollars.  \par \par \uc2 The reserves had ballooned after yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of a record 20 trillion yen ($192.3 billion) in 2003 and a further 15 trillion yen in the first three months of 2004. However, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market since mid-March last year.  \par \par \uc2 The MOF has estimated, based on various assumptions at the time, that unrealised losses on its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  account could rise to around 11.4 trillion yen ($109.5 billion) by the end of March next year.  \par \par \uc2 The MOF, however, insists that Japan's reserves are not necessarily loss-making despite the dollar's decline, saying interest rate differentials between the yen and other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  offset, or at least dampen, the exchange rate losses.  \par \par \uc2 Data for the fiscal year to March 2004 show that on a flow basis, gross income from the reserves stood at 1.7 trillion yen, more than double the cost for interest payment of 7.4 billion.  \par \par \uc2 The current market yield on the MOF's three-month finance bills, the main debt instrument for its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  account, is about 0.004 percent per annum, compared with rates on three-month eurodollar bank deposits of 2.98 percent and three-month U.S. Treasury bills of 2.69 percent. (Additional reporting by Tamawa Kadoya, Eric Burroughs and Chisa Fujioka, and Shinichi Kishima) ($1=103.98 Yen).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050310e13a003vl\par }\page {\par\fs20{