\par\par \b \uc2 Japan Tanigaki: Must Be Very Cautious On  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Reserves  \b0\par\par\uc2 579 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2005\par 04:03\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japanese Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki said Thursday that the government must be very cautious when discussing the issue of the nation's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves portfolio.  \par \par \uc2 "With (\b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) reserves this large, we must consider various factors, and we must take serious consideration of the markets as well," Tanigaki told reporters after an Upper House budget committee session.  \par \par \uc2 "We have been very cautious in the decisions we have taken on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves," he added.  \par \par \uc2 Tanigaki was speaking shortly after Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi told a parliamentary committee that in general, it is better to diversify investments.  \par \par \uc2 The finance minister said the prime minister's remarks may have been taken as meaning there has been a change in Japan's policy regarding foreign reserves.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar fell against the euro and the yen following Koizumi's remarks. The dollar, which was trading above Y104.00 before the comments, tumbled to a low of Y103.70, while the euro rose to $1.3458 from just around $1.3400.  \par \par \uc2 But the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regained ground a bit after that. As of 0316 GMT, it was trading at Y104.02 and $1.3409.  \par \par \uc2 -By James Simms, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; james.simms@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Hugh Lawson and Shawn Schroter [ 10-03-05 0322GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japanese Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki said Friday that the nation's massive \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves should be held as they are now, ruling out the possibility of selling off any of its reserve holdings.  \par \par \uc2 Tanigaki, speaking before the Upper House Budget Committee, said that Japan's reserves have grown large as a result of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's past yen-selling, dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 But when asked if \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had any plans to sell off its reserve assets, such as any of its U.S. Treasury holdings, Tanigaki said that it was best to hold the reserves as they are.  \par \par \uc2 "It is meaningful to keep holding our reserves, as they can be used as future \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  funding," Tanigaki said, referring to reserves as necessary funding for any yen-buying, dollar-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's government can finance yen-selling, dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  borrowing yen through the issuance of financing bills.  \par \par \uc2 But in the event it wants to conduct yen-buying, dollar-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the yen's weakness, it must sell dollars held in its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves. [ 11-03-05 0651GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Tanigaki said that Japan's reserves have grown to such proportions due to the MOF's past yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "As our \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was mostly dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , our reserves have built up," Tanigaki said.  \par \par \uc2 But he repeated that it was necessary to exercise caution when discussing reserves, given the effect such talk can have on financial markets.  \par \par \uc2 Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, the largest in the world, totaled $840.56 billion at the end of February. While the ministry doesn't reveal the actual breakdown of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  holdings, the reserves are believed to be held mostly in U.S. dollar-denominated assets, such as Treasurys.  \par \par \uc2 Tanigaki's comments Friday are in line with those by other MOF officials in recent days saying that Tokyo has no plans to shift its dollar holdings into other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , such as the euro.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Miyako Takebe, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; miyako.takebe@dowjones.com \par    -Edited by Shawn Schroter \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 11-03-05 0655GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020050310e13a0005i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
