\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Japan's Watanabe says to act on abrupt  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  moves.  \b0\par\par\uc2 323 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2005\par 06:50\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  TOKYO, March 8 (Reuters) - Japan's top financial diplomat, Hiroshi Watanabe, said on Tuesday the yen may still be overvalued and that authorities were ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market moved abruptly.   \par \par \uc2  "Of course, if there are some very abrupt moves and overshooting, we will (act) in the market," Watanabe, vice finance minister for international affairs, told foreign correspondents when asked when \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  might resume its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The government has stayed out of the market for nearly a year since its aggressive dollar-buying campaign in the 15 months to March 2004.   \par \par \uc2  Japanese authorities sold a record 20 trillion yen ($190.4 billion) in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in calendar 2003 and another 15 trillion yen in the first three months of 2004, fearing the yen's excessive strength could hurt the economy.   \par \par \uc2  Watanabe said market conditions had improved since 2003 when there was a "one-way sentiment for the depreciation of the dollar."   \par \par \uc2  He added: "Even though there are concerns that still the Japanese yen is overvalued, today I have no intention to make any verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."   \par \par \uc2  The dollar ticked up slightly to 105.07 yen  after those comments. It traded at around 104.95 yen earlier in the day.   \par \par \uc2  The last time the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped into the market was on March 16 last year, when the dollar fell close to two yen on the day to around 108.60 yen.   \par \par \uc2  Watanabe also reiterated the government's stance that China should eventually adopt a more flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime, but did not specify a time frame or method.   \par \par \uc2  He said that having a flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime was crucial for such an economic giant in handling external and internal shocks. A lack of a "workable money market" meant it may be less capable of dealing with potential problems such as its growing pension system, he said. ($1=105.06 Yen).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050308e138004h8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
