\par\par \b \uc2 Japan Watanabe: To Act If  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Moves Abrupt, Overshoot  \b0\par\par\uc2 758 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2005\par 05:26\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japan will have to take action if \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates move abruptly or overshoot, but the current stable market situation doesn't warrant such steps, Japan's top \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bureaucrat Hiroshi Watanabe said Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 "While the yen is still overvalued against other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market situation recently seems to be corresponding to economic indicators more than in the past," Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Watanabe told reporters at a luncheon hosted by the Foreign Correspondents' Club of Japan.  \par \par \uc2 That is a "natural way" for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates to move, and matches an agreement among Group of Seven leading industrial nations that \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves should reflect economic fundamentals, Watanabe said.  \par \par \uc2 "But if there are abrupt movements, or if the market overshoots, we are very much ready to act," Watanabe said. "I hope the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market doesn't force me to act." [ 08-03-05 0447GMT ]  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has refrained from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market since last March, when it abruptly ended an unprecedented \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaign to stem the yen's rise against the dollar. In the 15 months from January 2003 to March 2004, the MOF spent a record Y35 trillion selling yen through the Bank of Japan.  \par \par \uc2 "Today I'm not here to celebrate the one-year anniversary of the end" of market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Watanabe said. "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be done according to the market situation" and overall economic conditions, he said.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves are "always relative," Watanabe said, adding that even if Japan's economy performs well, the dollar could rise more against the yen if the U.S. economy performs better.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates should move in such a way but weren't doing so in 2003, Watanabe said, defending the MOF's heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  back then.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is said to be a "beauty contest," but in 2003 it was a "less ugly" contest, Watanabe said, suggesting that the yen was bought against the dollar only because the market thought that Japan's conditions were less severe than those of the United States, which was suffering under its huge current account deficit.  \par \par \uc2 "I don't want to see the same thing again," he said, issuing a strong warning against any speculative attempts to push the yen higher.  \par \par \uc2 Watanabe was upbeat about the prospect of the United States tacking its huge current account and fiscal deficits, saying that he believes Washington has made a "very firm" commitment and will "definitely proceed" with steps to address it.  \par \par \uc2 On China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime, Watanabe said moving from a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg to a floating rate system would be "too extreme," given the still-underdeveloped state of China's money market.  \par \par \uc2 He said there are "alternatives" in achieving \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility, and that Japan will ask China to adopt a more flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime - but not say that the yuan should appreciate or depreciate.  \par \par \uc2 "I don't want to force China to adopt a floating system," he said, suggesting that it was up to Beijing to decide how best to achieve \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Leika Kihara and Miyako Takebe, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-9203; leika.kihara@dowjones.com, miyako.takebe@dowjones.com   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Kirsty Mackenzie and Bennett Richardson [ 08-03-05 0555GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has no plans to diversify the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  makeup of its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves, but could consider some diversity within its dollar-denominated assets, a MOF official said Thursday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We are not thinking of changing the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  within our reserves," the official told Dow Jones Newswires. He was speaking shortly after Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi told a Parliamentary committee that in general, it is better to diversify investments.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The MOF official said that Koizumi's remarks didn't refer to changes in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  but rather to having some diversity in the type of assets the government holds.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "For example, we could diversify within our dollar-denominated assets," he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar fell against the euro and the yen following Koizumi's remarks. The dollar, which was trading above Y104.00 before the comments, tumbled to a low of Y103.70, while the euro rose to $1.3458 from just around $1.3400.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  regained ground a bit after the MOF's clarifications. As of 0255 GMT, it was trading at Y103.94 and $1.3415.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Miyako Takebe, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; miyako.takebe@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Hugh Lawson [ 10-03-05 0256GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020050308e13800053\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
