\par\par \b \uc2 Yen firm in Europe after Japan data, BOJ comments.  \b0\par\par\uc2 232 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Februar 2005\par 08:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Feb 28 (Reuters) - The yen rose against the dollar and euro on Monday after robust Japanese industrial output data and hawkish comments from Bank of Japan governor Toshihiko Fukui raised optimism about the world's second largest economy.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese industrial output rose a higher-than-expected 2.1 percent in January from a month earlier.  \par \par \uc2 BOJ's Fukui warned financial markets against assuming that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's ultra-loose monetary policy will last indefinitely, although he reiterated the bank had no plans to alter its current policy.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar hit a seven-week low against the euro and a two-month low versus sterling. It also slipped towards a recent one-year low against the Australian dollar and a 22-year low on the New Zealand dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Traders cited everything from higher yields in Britain, Australia and New Zealand to a spike in oil prices and concerns about possible diversification by central banks out of dollars as reasons for the dollar's broad weakness.  \par \par \uc2 Japan's Finance Ministry releases data on how much \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  spent on yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in February at 1000 GMT but analysts expect the MOF stayed away from the market that month.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. January PCE (Personal Consumption Expenditure) core price index, closely watched by the Federal Reserve, is due at 1330 GMT.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050228e12s00591\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
