\par\par \b \uc2 WRAPUP 1-Asia in damage control after reserves jitters.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Yoo Choonsik  \par \uc2 841 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Februar 2005\par 12:58\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL, Feb 23 (Reuters) - Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities scurried to control damage on Wednesday after a South Korean document referring to diversification of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves sparked an across-the-board dollar sell-off against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 South Korea, whose reserves are the world's fourth largest at $200 billion, flatly denied diversification plans meant an exodus from the dollar, while Japan, the world's biggest holder of reserves, said it had no plans to fiddle with the composition of its funds.  \par \par \uc2 "The plan meant that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would diversify reserves more into non-government bonds and did not mean that it would sell current dollar holdings for other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," the Bank of Korea said in a statement on Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 It was referring to its report to parliament on Monday outlining its plans for 2005 that said it would diversify its reserves away from U.S. government bonds into higher yielding debt and into non-dollar \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The dollar logged its biggest daily fall this year on Tuesday, dropping to multi-week lows against the yen and the euro, seven-year lows against the Korean won and 22-year lows against the New Zealand dollar on the report.  \par \par \uc2 It retraced some losses on Wednesday after the Bank of Korea's clarifications and Japan's vehement denial of any changes to its reserves policy.  \par \par \uc2 "We have no plan to change the composition of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  holdings in the foreign reserves and we are not thinking about expanding our euro holdings," Masatsugu Asakawa, director of the Japanese finance ministry's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market division, told Reuters.  \par \par \uc2 Irked by the market's off-and-on speculation about their reserves, Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of China said in a statement that it had not been selling U.S. dollars.  \par \par \uc2 "The foreign media reports are inconsistent with facts. This press statement has been issued to set the record right," the bank said.  \par \par \uc2 Japan, China, Taiwan and South Korea are the world's top holders of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, with nearly $2 trillion in their coffers combined.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 The turmoil highlights the growing risks stemming from the Asian authorities' bulging dollar holdings resulting from years of heavy market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent export-crimping rises in their national \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  alone spent a record 20 trillion yen ($191.6 billion) in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2003 and a further 15 trillion yen in just the first three months of last year before suddenly quitting such market action.  \par \par \uc2 The yen rose to five-year highs against the dollar last month, sparking market expectations Tokyo authorities might renew \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Bloated dollar holdings in reserves means huge exposure to the risk of losses should the dollar fall, but trying to reduce them would cause the dollar to fall even more sharply.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki, in an interview with Reuters earlier this month, said Tokyo had to be extremely careful in managing its reserves because of their sheer size. Japan's reserves totalled $844.5 billion at the end of 2004.  \par \par \uc2 The biggest chunk of that pile is in U.S. dollars and any change to the composition could have an immense impact on exchange rates, particularly as Asia's purchases of dollar assets have effectively funded the United States' gaping current account and budget deficits.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts say a diversification to non-dollar \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  was inevitable, not the least because of a growing acceptance of the euro, the European single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that was born in 1999. Likewise, U.S. Treasuries are no longer seen as the only safe-haven investment.  \par \par \uc2 The State Administration of \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 , China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulator, denied in December China was cutting U.S. dollar assets held in its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, saying it would not make changes based on short-term market moves.  \par \par \uc2 But U.S. government data suggest Asian countries were diversifying assets to some extent, as their combined holdings of U.S. Treasury securities rose more slowly in 2004 than the rise in total reserves.  \par \par \uc2 China's holdings of U.S. Treasuries rose 23 percent in 2004 while its external reserves jumped 51 percent, although the figures include Treasury holdings by the private sector.  \par \par \uc2 "Suggestions that China is selling U.S. Treasuries are not correct, which have been denied by the Chinese government. The absolute amount of Treasury holdings are still rising," said Jun Ma, an economist at Deutsche Bank in Hong Kong.  \par \par \uc2 "(But) the purchases of U.S. Treasuries in the incremental part of China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves have been declining."  \par \par \uc2 Some analysts believe China has boosted its holdings of euros and yen to dilute dollar assets, which they estimate at 70-80 percent of the total reserves.  \par \par \uc2 The make-up of external reserves remains a secret in many countries, though South Korea has been saying publicly over the last few years it wanted to diversify over time. ($1=104.38 Yen) (Additional reporting by Yoko Nishikawa in Tokyo, Kevin Yao in Beijing and Lee Chyen Yee in Taipei).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050223e12n008kg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
