\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Japan reiterates no plan to switch reserves to euro.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Yoko Nishikawa  \par \uc2 525 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Februar 2005\par 05:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Feb 23 (Reuters) - Japan reiterated on Wednesday that it has no plans to divert its massive foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, the world's largest, out of dollars and into euros.  \par \par \uc2 "We have no plan to change the composition of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  holdings in the foreign reserves and we are not thinking about expanding our euro holdings," Masatsugu Asakawa, director of the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market division at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF), told Reuters.  \par \par \uc2 After the comment, the euro fell to around $1.3230 from session highs close to $1.3270, while the dollar hit session highs against the yen near 104.80 yen after starting Asian trade around 104 yen.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar had fallen to multi-week lows against most other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  after South Korea's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Monday it would diversify its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, Asia's fourth-largest at $200 billion, away from government bonds into high yielding paper and a greater variety of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 But the dollar rebounded against the euro and the yen on Wednesday after South Korea signalled that plans to diversify its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves were not new and did not mean it would sell the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Japan has had the world's largest foreign reserves for more than five years. At the end of January, the reserves stood at $840.966 billion, of which $696.978 billion was in securities and $124.225 billion in cash. The rest includes IMF reserve positions and gold.  \par \par \uc2 It does not give a breakdown of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the reserves. But given \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's massive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the past, in which it sold yen for dollars, the reserves are believed to be predominantly in dollars.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese authorities sold a record 20 trillion yen ($192 billion) in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2003 and another 15 trillion yen in the first three months of 2004 in an attempt to halt what they saw as an excessive rise in the yen.  \par \par \uc2 Excessive yen strength is seen as bad for Japanese exporters, as it can cut into the value of their overseas profits or make their goods less competitive in global markets, and could accelerate deflation, which has plagued the economy for more than five years.  \par \par \uc2 Tokyo has not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market for almost a year, having last conducted dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on March 16 last year.  \par \par \uc2 The ministry has repeatedly said it will not diversify the reserves out of dollars into other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , such as euros, since such action could precipitate a further fall in the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 But it has been considering diversifying its portfolio management, including possible use of private-sector firms as agents to bid in U.S. Treasury auctions, as part of efforts to more efficiently manage the huge reserves.  \par \par \uc2 Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki told Reuters in an interview earlier this month that Japan will be careful how it manages its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves due to the potential impact on financial markets.  \par \par \uc2 Tanigaki said the basic principle is that the reserves should be stable and liquid. ($1=104.15 Yen).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050223e12n003pf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
