\par\par \b \uc2 G7 sticks to  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  policy, China doesn't budge.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Brian Love, European Economics Correspondent  \par \uc2 704 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Februar 2005\par 20:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Feb 5 (Reuters) - The G7 wealthy nations on Saturday predicted solid economic growth and subdued inflation this year while repeating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  declarations that some economists believe could limit the dollar's decline.  \par \par \uc2 China, invited to Group of Seven talks, came under renewed pressure to ease its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg to the dollar but said it had no timetable for action on G7 complaints that it keeps the yuan too low, making life hard for other trading nations.  \par \par \uc2 Stephen Jen, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Morgan Stanley, said there was nothing surprising in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  statement published after the meeting, but that some of the ministers' comments showed they wanted the dollar to go no lower.  \par \par \uc2 "I think the dollar is going to have a good period now," Jen said after the Group of Seven meeting ended in London.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  massively a year ago to keep the yen from getting too strong versus the dollar and hurting its exports and a hesitant recovery from a decade in the economic doldrums.  \par \par \uc2 But it has so far resisted the temptation to do so again.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. interest rates were rising and what seemed to be a more serious commitment to deficit reduction were dollar-supportive and likely to captivate financial markets more than the deficit worries that drove the dollar to a record low against the euro in December, Jen said.  \par \par \uc2 "People are going to start looking more at rate divergence and on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  politics it seems (U.S. President George W.) Bush and (Treasury Secretary John) Snow sound a little stronger now on the strong dollar policy," Jen said.  \par \par \uc2 Snow did not attend the London meeting of finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chiefs because of a cold and sent Treasury Under Secretary John Taylor in his place.  \par \par \uc2 The communique carried a paragraph on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  which was a word-for-word repeat of the formula the G7 produced a year ago in Boca Raton, Florida - in essence saying they wanted less volatility and more flexibility, the latter as a hint to China.  \par \par \uc2 "This is exactly what the market was expecting, said Ian Stannard, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at BNP Paribas in London.  \par \par \uc2 "The dollar is likely to see its recent trend continue as the focus of the market is very much on cyclicals which have been providing it with some support," he said.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar's slide accelerated in the last quarter of 2004 when it was hitting five-year lows versus the yen and a record low versus the euro above $1.36 per euro.  \par \par \uc2 "They did a good job of managing expectations, especially regarding the statement. It's basically a non-event." Daniel Katzive, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at UBS, said.  \par \par \uc2 NO MOVEMENT FOR NOW FROM CHINA  \par \par \uc2 Chinese Finance Minister Jin Renqing and Beijing's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bosses were invited to talk to G7 ministers.  \par \par \uc2 "We are determined to move towards a flexible exchange rate, but no timetable," Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  deputy governor Li Ruogu told reporters.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Zhou Xiaochuan, adding that economic growth this year might beat an 8 percent forecast after 9.5 percent growth in 2004. That is more than double the U.S. forecast and four times that of the euro zone.  \par \par \uc2 "The comments from China are consistent with what they have been saying," said UBS's Katzive.  \par \par \uc2 "They are moving forward with \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reform but there was not the big move some were expecting. I still think there is the chance of a small move from the Chinese sometime this year."  \par \par \uc2 The theory is that if China eases its peg to the dollar, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will rise and take the pressure off others in Asia to keep their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  down to protect exports competitiveness.  \par \par \uc2 A senior U.S. official sounded an optimistic note on the likelihood of a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy change by China after Taylor met the Chinese. "Most pieces are in place and largely ready to go in that direction," the official said.  \par \par \uc2 Italian Economy Minister Domenico Siniscalco said talks with the Chinese had focussed on the economy and what references there were to monetary policy were very general and "without brusque questions or precise answers".   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050205e12500c9p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
