\par\par \b \uc2 G7:MoF Tanigaki:Japan Maintains  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Volatility Unwelcome  \b0\par\par\uc2 465 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Februar 2005\par 17:29\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 LONDON (Dow Jones)--Japan maintains that \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign- exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates should reflect fundamentals and that excessive \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market movements are undesirable, Japanese Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki said Saturday.  \par \par \uc2 "Our \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy is conducted under this basic stance, which is also based on the G7's understanding on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Tanigaki said.  \par \par \uc2 Tanigaki was speaking after finance ministers and central bankers from the Group of Seven leading industrialized nations, in their final communique released after two days of meetings here, repeated language they first adopted at their meeting in Boca Raton, Florida, in February 2004.  \par \par \uc2 "Excess volatility and disorderly movements in exchange rates are undesirable for economic growth," the G7 said. "We emphasize that more flexibility in exchange rates is desirable for major countries...that lack such flexibility."  \par \par \uc2 Tanigaki said even though the G7 maintained the same language on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , that didn't mean that they were condoning the dollar's fall against the euro and the yen, which had occurred since October on fears of the growing U.S. current account deficit.  \par \par \uc2 "Such a view is mistaken," he said, pointing out the G7 agreed that exchange rates should reflect economic fundamentals, and that the U.S. has pledged to pursue fiscal consolidation. [ 05-02-05 1708GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Tanigaki added that there was no talk of Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy at the just-ended G7 meeting.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has refrained from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets since last March, when it ended an unprecedented \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaign to stem the yen's rise against the dollar. In the 15 months from January 2003 to March 2004, the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  spent a record Y35 trillion selling yen through the Bank of Japan.  \par \par \uc2 Tanigaki's comments suggest that despite the communique's call for more flexibility in exchange rates, Tokyo reserves the right to resume \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  if it feels movements are excessive given the G7's aversion to disorderly movements.  \par \par \uc2 Tanigaki also said he told his G7 counterparts that Japan's economy would probably post moderate growth in 2005.  \par \par \uc2 "Japan's economy, although currently in an adjustment phase, remains on an upward path," Tanigaki said.  \par \par \uc2 He added that he believes the G7 was able to make progress on addressing global structural imbalances, with the U.S., Japan and Europe all committing to further their respective efforts.  \par \par \uc2 "We shared the view that we need to work toward sustaining global growth, and in that sense, it was a step forward," Tanigaki said.  \par \par \uc2 Tanigaki also said the G7 was making efforts to come up with measures to help African nations meet their Millennium Development Goal, adding that he explained Tokyo's aid plans to the G7.  \par \par \uc2 -By Miyako Takebe, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; miyako.takebe@dowjones.com [ 05-02-05 1738GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020050205e125000a3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
