\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL YIELD:Peering Nervously At Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Bid  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,058 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Februar 2005\par 22:08\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Agnes T. Crane \par    A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan proclaimed Friday what fixed income investors have been saying for more than a year: official buying from Asia has helped keep U.S. yields low.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 The problem is, more and more evidence suggests the large Asian central banks may be losing their appetite for Treasurys, raising the bar for the looming U.S. government quarterly refunding and fueling questions about how high yields will go when Asian central banks retreat.  \par \par \uc2 Specifically speaking on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Asian central banks in which they buy dollars to prevent their own \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from rising, Greenspan told a London audience this "may be supporting the dollar and U.S. Treasury bond prices somewhat." He of course added that the magnitude of the effect on interest rates and the buck "is difficult to pin down."  \par \par \uc2 Bond market watchers have come up with some estimates of their own. Many reckon the benchmark 10-year note's yield would be some 0.5 percentage point higher, were it not for the strong bid from official Asian investors.  \par \par \uc2 Japan has stood head and shoulders above the other Asian central banks in terms of channeling dollars purchased in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets into Treasury bonds. According to analysts at UBS, purchases by the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  accounted for roughly half of all the Treasurys bought by foreign investors last year.  \par \par \uc2 Though the Japanese government hasn't bought a single dollar since last March, it was able to gradually channel the stash of dollars accumulated during the last bout of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  into Treasurys through August. In September, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Treasury holdings fell for the first time in nearly two years.  \par \par \uc2 Other Asian central banks picked up some of the slack in the fourth quarter of last year, but recent data released from the Fed and anecdotal evidence from Treasury auctions hint that the appetite of these other banks also may be waning.  \par \par \uc2 Last week, a collective gulp was heard across trading desks when the Treasury Department reported that indirect bidders, which include foreign central banks as well as institutional investors, bought less than 30% of the $24 billion of two-year notes sold at auction. The Federal Reserve Bank of New York then reported Thursday that the Treasurys it holds in custody for foreign central banks dropped by an eye-popping $12.9 billion in the week ending Feb. 2.  \par \par \uc2 Lou Crandall, economist at Jersey City-based Wrightson ICAP, who keeps close tabs on the data, sat up and took notice. "Not only is this the largest one-week decline since the turmoil following the Russian default in August 1998, but it occurred in a week in which foreign official holdings of Treasuries usually rise," he noted. Weeks that include Treasury auctions or refundings typically show a noticeable increase in custody holdings, he said.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par      Staring Down Supply Barrel \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 To be sure, there could be other explanations for the outright decline. For example, if a foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  wanted to lend out some of its securities to investors needing to cover positions, this would show up as a temporary dent in their holdings. "Still, with the February refunding auctions looming next week, the fall-off in foreign holdings will look ominous to the market," Crandall said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors are staring down the barrel of a pipeline that will deliver $51 billion in new Treasury notes as part of the government's refunding package. The Treasury will sell $22 billion of three-year notes, $15 billion of five-year notes and $14 billion of ten-year notes. While the total amount was unchanged from the previous quarter's refunding, the new cash it will raise at $39.6 billion is the largest amount Wall Street has seen since March 2003.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 If last week's two-year note is any gauge of demand, primary dealers, those banks that must participate in the auctions, could be in for a rude awakening.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It's a lot of new money to take down" for the primary dealers, who must bid for the new notes, said Gerald Lucas, head of Treasury and agency strategy at Banc of America Securities in New York. The drop in custody holdings "isn't a good omen."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 And in some respects, the timing couldn't be worse. On Friday, another disappointing nonfarm payroll report caused a reluctant rally in the Treasury market, sending the 10-year benchmark yield to its lowest level this year and the 30-year yield down to a trough not seen since June 2003.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Few domestic investors are happy buying Treasurys at such expensive levels, particularly when the majority of portfolio managers have been positioned for lower prices and thus higher yields. But given the market's positive performance since the beginning of the year, it's become more difficult for portfolio managers to resist the momentum in the marketplace.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Craig Coats, head of fixed income at Keefe, Bruyette & Woods in New York, said many have kept a good portion of their funds in cash waiting for yields to finally move higher in 2005. "We are now halfway through the first quarter and the data (Friday) was not indicative of the market about to fall apart," he said. "You can't stay in overnight cash and still have earnings."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Earlier Friday, the Labor Department reported U.S. employers added 146,000 to nonfarm payrolls in January, less than the 200,000 that Wall Street economists had expected. The knee-jerk reaction to the slower than expected pace of hiring sparked a round of selling of Treasurys which then snowballed into a full-blown rally as some of that cash finally moved into government securities.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Lucas, of Banc of America, expects yields to back up next week to make room for the refunding, but added that the rally combined with a possible falloff in foreign interest for Treasurys could make for messy auctions where dealers struggle to place the new paper into willing hands.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "If you are nervous about next week's auctons, you should be," Lucas said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Agnes T. Crane covers global credit markets for Dow Jones Newswires).  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Agnes T. Crane, Dow Jones Newswires, 201-938-2122; \par agnes.crane@dowjones.com \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 02-04-05 1608ET ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 4083  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020050204e1240003n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
