\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  says did not  \highlight22\uc2 intervene \highlight\uc2  in  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  market in Jan.  \b0\par\par\uc2 457 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Januar 2005\par 11:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Jan 31 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) said on Monday it did not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in January, a month that saw the yen rise to a five-year high against the U.S. dollar.  \par \par \uc2 MOF data showed the ministry stayed away from the market from Dec. 29 to Jan. 27 - its tenth straight month of inaction since an aggressive dollar buying campaign from January to mid-March last year to protect Japan's export-led recovery.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar fell to a five-year low of 101.67 yen on Jan. 17, threatening to break below the psychologically important 100 yen mark, pressured by worries about the ability of the United States to finance its swelling current account deficit.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has bounced back since then and fetched around 103.50 yen in Tokyo trade on Monday, supported by expectations that the U.S. Federal Reserve will press on with its campaign of "measured" interest rate rises.  \par \par \uc2 Many traders, however, still expect the dollar to continue its descent this year, possibly making it tough for Japanese authorities, who fear too strong a yen will hurt Japanese exporters' overseas profits and derail the economy.  \par \par \uc2 Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  including the yen are expected to rise in unison when China relaxes the peg of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the dollar - a move that some expect as early as the next few months.  \par \par \uc2 Japan's Vice Finance Minister Koichi Hosokawa on Monday reiterated his ministry's well-worn position that Japan would take appropriate action in \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets if needed.  \par \par \uc2 But many traders think \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unlikely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  soon - even if the dollar falls below the 100 yen mark, a line it has not crossed since late 1995.  \par \par \uc2 They say that if China let the yuan appreciate and other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  followed, it would likely be difficult for Tokyo to justify massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stifle the yen's rise.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts also point out that despite the surge in the yen, Japanese companies are heading for a third straight year of record profits in the period to March.  \par \par \uc2 MOF data last week also showed that partly due to rapid growth in trade with Asian nations like China, a record 40 percent of Japanese exports are now paid for in yen, which provides corporate Japan with a buffer against \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swings.  \par \par \uc2 The last time Japan stepped into the market was on March 16, 2004, when the dollar fell close to two yen on the day to around 108.60 yen.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese authorities spent a record 20 trillion yen ($192.7 billion) on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2003 and another 15 trillion yen in the first three months of 2004.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050131e11v00b46\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
