\par\par \b \uc2 Japan seen in dilemma over  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  at G7.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Chisa Fujioka  \par \uc2 647 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Januar 2005\par 10:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Jan 31 (Reuters) - Japan will be caught between calls for more flexibility in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and its own worries about a higher yen when the Group of Seven meets this week amid speculation China would soon revalue the yuan, analysts say.  \par \par \uc2 That speculation sent the yen to five-year highs earlier this month, and remarks by Chinese authorities on yuan policy have sparked knee-jerk buying and selling in the yen in recent sessions.  \par \par \uc2 While Japan too backs the G7's stance that China should eventually relax the yuan's peg to the dollar, it could grow uncomfortable with the yen increasingly being used by investors as a proxy to bet on any across-the-board rise in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  seen resulting from a change in yuan policy.  \par \par \uc2 Many analysts do not expect China to reform its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime anytime soon. But markets have remained nervous as G7 finance ministers and central bankers were expected to shed light on the issue at a meeting in London on Friday and Saturday.  \par \par \uc2 "Japan's basic view is that a revaluation of the yuan is needed to a certain extent, but it'll be a dilemma if it were to result in a higher yen," said Masaaki Kanno, chief economist at JP Morgan in Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 A revaluation of the yuan could remove \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pegs across the region and could put an end to \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  competitive.  \par \par \uc2 "Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the yen, won and rupee will probably appreciate in the run-up (to yuan revaluation), though it's difficult to say by how much," said Eisuke Sakakibara, a former Japanese vice finance minister for international affairs.  \par \par \uc2 A higher yen makes Japanese products less competitive in global markets, which would be a major setback for the country's export-reliant economy.  \par \par \uc2 Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki signalled last week that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  remained ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , vowing to act if needed against any "unnatural" \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves.  \par \par \uc2 Japan spent a record 35 trillion yen ($337.6 billion) on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the 15 months to last March to slow an appreciation of the yen.  \par \par \uc2 MINDFUL OF EUROPE  \par \par \uc2 Japan's concerns may be muted for now, however, with the yen off a five-year high of 101.67 to the dollar hit on Jan. 17 and far from strong in trade-weighted terms.  \par \par \uc2 Japan is also seen mindful of European officials, who have made no secret of their desire to see Asian authorities stop meddling with their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and share more of a burden in slimming the huge U.S. deficit.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese officials have played down concerns of rifts within the G7, saying the group was committed to its year-old statement that more flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates were desirable in countries where flexibility was lacking.  \par \par \uc2 "I checked with a few G7 officials from other countries and they have said this reference does not include Japan," Makoto Utsumi, Japan's top \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  official from 1989-1991, told Reuters.  \par \par \uc2 But European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB) governing council member Nout Wellink noted last week that Japan, China and some other Asian countries had built up "unprecedented high dollar reserves" due to policies of stabilising \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 ECB chief economist Otmar Issing also suggested earlier this month that Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were more able to shoulder the burden of the dollar's broad weakness, comments which sent the yen sharply higher.  \par \par \uc2 JP Morgan's Kanno said headaches for Japanese authorities could grow if a yuan revaluation and a higher yen coincided with a downturn in the Japanese economy and a slide in its stock market, raising the urgency for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will have a hard time justifying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  once the yuan is revalued, because they could then be the only one in the region doing it," he said. ($1=103.66 Yen).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050131e11v00ai0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
