\par\par \b \uc2 Dow Jones Asia-Pacific Top Stories Sked For Jan 31  \b0\par\par\uc2 2,998 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Januar 2005\par 10:26\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 Following are the top Asia-Pacific stories featured on Dow Jones Newswires as of 0900 GMT, along with items expected later in the day. Please direct any questions or comments to asianewseds@dowjones.com or by phone on 65-6415-4020.  \par \par \uc2 MACRO/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /FIXED INCOME  \par \par \uc2 MANILA: The Philippine economy grew at its fastest pace in 15 years, rising 6.1% last year because of a steadily expanding services sector and robust consumer spending. ("=UPDATE: Philippine 2004 GDP Up 6.1%; Services Give Boost," ran at 0532 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: The time isn't ripe for China to revalue the yuan as the country hasn't yet decided on a suitable \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  system, a National Statistics Bureau spokesman said. ("China Stats Bureau: Time Not Ripe For Yuan Revaluation," first take ran at 0322 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Asia: Japanese officials going to meet their Group of Seven colleagues in London this weekend are unlikely to linger too long on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  issues, as the existing G7 view on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  meshes well with the policies of a slightly more-relaxed Tokyo. ("=\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Asia: Mellower Japan To Settle For G7 Status Quo," ran at 0730 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: A suspected embezzlement case at a Bank of China branch in northeastern China that involves a roadway company's funds, plus social security funds, may total more than CNY1 billion, the official Xinhua News Agency said in a report carried in state newspapers Monday. ("Bank Of China Fraud May Be Worth Over CNY1 Bln - Xinhua," ran at 0103 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Korea Development Bank has raised the price of its EUR500 million in international bonds now on offer to 32 to 34 basis points over the Euro interbank offered rate, a person familiar with the deal said Monday. ("Korea KDB EUR Bond Guidance Now Euribor+32-34BPS -Source," ran at 0855 GMT; bond to price later Monday)  \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK: Thailand's trade surplus widened in December to its highest level since March 2000 after imports slumped on reduced demand for capital goods. ("=DATA SNAP:Thai Dec Trade Surplus Widens On Weak Imports," ran at 0826 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Singapore's unemployment rate fell to 3.7% in the fourth quarter from a year ago, but rose slightly from the previous quarter due to layoffs, data released Monday show. ("Singapore 4Q Unemployment Drops To 3.7% Vs 4.6% On Year," first take ran at 0354 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: New Zealand's December goods trade deficit reduced from the previous year and was lower than expectations, according to official data released Monday. ("DATA SNAP: NZ December Goods Trade Deficit NZ$493M" ran at 2208 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: China is to continue its reforms of the country's agricultural sector, including a further widening of its program to abolish rural taxes, a senior government bureaucrat said Monday. (Story to run by around 1115 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Against the backdrop of the Link fiasco, the Hong Kong government is extending the public consultation period for the privatization of the city's international airport to ensure all outstanding issues are resolved. ("HK Govt Extends Airport Sale Consultation Period," ran at 0711 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 KUALA LUMPUR: Malaysian Prime Minister Abdullah Ahmad Badawi Monday said businessman Syed Mokhtar's purchase of a stake in DRB-Hicom Bhd. is on track. ("Malaysia's PM: Syed Mokhtar's DRB-Hicom Buy On Track," first take ran at 0528 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: The Shanghai Futures Exchange said the country's qualified foreign institutional investor program could be expanded to include futures, particularly in copper. ("Shanghai Futures Exchange Sees QFII Aiding Copper Futures," ran at 0110 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 NEW DELHI: The Indian government Monday revised upward its estimate of the country's gross domestic product growth for the last fiscal year to 8.5% from an earlier projection of 8.2%. ("India Last FY GDP +8.5% Vs +8.2% Earlier Estimate," ran at 0650 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 NEW DELHI: Government to release fiscal deficit figures for the April-December period. (Story to run around 1100 GMT, update possible).  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: Australia's Export Finance and Insurance Corp. has tapped the Japanese uridashi bond market for the first time in six months with a NZ$30 million two-year high-coupon bond. ("REPEAT:Australian Export Agency Issues NZD Uridashi Again," ran at 0323 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  to release data on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in January. (Story to run around 1000 GMT).  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  OPTION VIEWS: The potential for turbulence in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is building with the Group of Seven industrial nations meeting just days away. Owning one-year dollar/yen options in anticipation of a technical break remains an attractive trade. ("=\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  OPTION VIEWS: One-Year USD/JPY Vol At A Crossroads," ran at 0602 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Equitable PCI Bank, the third largest lender in the Philippines, is starting roadshows Monday for a $100 million to $150 million three-year bond, a person familiar with the deal said. ("Philippine Equitable PCI Seeks $100M-$150M 3-Year Bond" ran at 0146 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 EQUITIES  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Toshiba Corp. said Monday it returned to profitability on a group net basis in the October-December quarter, but it trimmed its full-year forecasts mainly due to sluggish chip \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . (UPDATE including TDK, Sanyo Electric, Pioneer earnings to run by around 1100 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: All Nippon Airways Co. said Monday its group net profit for the April-December period rose 28% from a year earlier, reflecting a pickup in international travel demand and cost cuts. (UPDATE to run by around 1030 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Takeda Pharmaceutical Co. said Monday its group net profit for the October-December quarter dropped 14% on year to Y82.44 billion despite a 2.4% rise in sales to Y304.34 billion. (UPDATE inclucing Sankyo, Fujisawa Pharmaceutical earnings to run by around 1100 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: China's Semiconductor Manufacturing International Corp. said Monday its sales grew to $291.8 million in the fourth quarter last year from $274.9 million in the previous quarter as the company engaged 19 new customers in the final quarter of 2004. (UPDATE to run shortly)  \par \par \uc2 KUALA LUMPUR: Malaysian Airline System Bhd. plans to expand its fleet with an order for up to 78 new aircraft this year, its chief financial officer Low Chee Teng said Monday. ("=INTERVIEW:Malaysian Air To Order Up To 78 Planes This Yr," ran at 0811 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: Spurred by its multibillion dollar deal with Chinese airlines, Boeing Co. Monday said it will continue targeting the Asia-Pacific region as it markets the Boeing 787 Dreamliner. ("=Boeing Targets Asia As Lead Market In Wake Of China Deal," ran at 0824 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Mizuho Financial Group Inc. Monday reported a hefty group net profit of Y623.64 billion for the April-December period thanks to a Y280 billion special profit from its winning a court case against the tax authority. ("REPEAT:Mizuho FG April-December Group Net Profit Y623.64B," ran at 0850 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: After failing in a bid to build a major toll road in Australia's second largest city Melbourne, Transurban Group Ltd. said Monday it will make a A$1.8 billion takeover offer for Hills Motorway Group Ltd., owner of Sydney's M2. ("=UPDATE: Transurban To Make A$1.8B Bid For Hills Motorway," ran at 0611 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MELBOURNE: Australian takeover target Virgin Blue Ltd. on Monday said that suitor Patrick Corp. may be legally required to lift its current offer for the discount carrier. ("Australia's Patrick May Have To Lift Bid - Virgin Blue," first take ran at 2329 GMT; see also "Singapore Air Says Not Bidding For Australia Virgin Blue" at 0505 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI: India's Oil & Natural Gas Corp. to release third-quarter earnings. (Timing unclear; Update likely)  \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI: India's Tata Motors to release third-quarter earnings. (Timing unclear; Update possible)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: South Korea plans to launch a new stock index by integrating the Korea Composite Stock Price Index and tech-heavy Kosdaq Index in the first half of 2005, the newly-formed Korea Exchange said Monday. ("S Korea To Launch New Integrated Stk Index First Half '05," ran at 0330 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =Asia Fund View: HSBC Asset Management is most bullish over Hong Kong shares in Asia, the only market in the region where it is overweight, and cautious over South Korea, Indonesia and India stocks. ("=Asia Fund View: HSBC Asset Mgmt Favors HK Stks In Asia," ran at 0550 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: South Korea's KT&G Corp. Monday said its 2004 net profit climbed 3% on year, driven by an increase in sales of higher-priced and low-tar cigarettes. ("S Korea KT&G 2004 Net KRW472.3B Vs KRW459.7B," ran at 0236 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: South Korea's biggest casualty and property insurer Samsung Fire & Marine Insurance Co. said Monday third-quarter earnings more than doubled on an improved loss ratio and lower bad loans. ("S Korea Samsung Fire 3Q Net KRW49.9B Vs KRW19.1B," first take ran at 0443 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Toyota Motor Corp. said Monday it plans to bolster production capacity at an existing factory in Thailand in order to meet strong demand for its new multipurpose vehicles. ("Toyota To Up Multi-Use Car Output Capacity At Thai Plant," ran at 0352 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TAIPEI: Taiwanese chipmaker Winbond Electronics Corp. said Monday its capital expenditure is likely to total NT$30 billion in 2005. ("Taiwan's Winbond Sees 2005 Capex NT$30B Vs US$100M In 04," first take ran at 0608 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Matsushita Electric Industrial Co. is considering starting production earlier than planned at a plasma display panel plant under construction in Japan, in a move to secure a bigger share of the growing market for flat-screen televisions, a company spokesman said Monday. ("Matsushita Considers Early Launch Of Japan PDP Factory," ran at 0339 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: In a sign that new pricing rules for initial public offerings aren't working as well as hoped, the China Securities Regulatory Commission has warned securities firms to improve how they analyze corporate value. ("China Regulators Call For IPO Pricing Integrity - Report," ran at 0241 GMT) [ 31-01-05 0926GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 ASIA DRAM Report: Prices of dynamic random access memory chips have already started to fall ahead of the Lunar New Year, an important support season for prices since computer demand in China spikes as people give PCs as gifts. The early price declines indicate supply is more than ample to handle computer demand, and bodes ill for chip prices, which may reach new lows in mid-February. (Story to run by around 1130 GMT).  \par \par \uc2 =Asian Stock Focus: Will a revaluation of the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  help reduce America's bloated trade deficit with China? A close look at one shoemaker's cost and pricing pressures show why that's probably wishful thinking. ("=Asian Stock Focus: Yue Yuen Shows Revaluation Limits," ran at 0342 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: A spokeswoman for HSBC Holdings PLC Monday declined to confirm a statement from a company in northern China that the two have set a new investment banking arrangement, saying the Chinese firm made the announcement "unilaterally" and that the British banking group would have its own statement later. ("HSBC Won't Confirm China Investment Bank Plans," ran at 0654 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: Macquarie Bank Ltd., Australia's largest listed investment bank, Monday said it plans to list a A$104 million private equity fund on the Australian Stock Exchange. ("=UPDATE: Macquarie Bank To List Private Equity Fund," ran at 0649 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MELBOURNE: National Australia Bank Ltd. Chairman Graham Kraehe said Monday that the nation's biggest bank remains in the early stage of its recovery phase following an unacceptable performance for shareholders in 2004. (UPDATE to run shortly)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Sojitz Holdings Corp. Monday reported a huge group net loss for the April-December period, as the heavily-indebted trading house undertakes a radical makeover announced last year. ("=UPDATE:Sojitz Apr-Dec Grp Net Loss Y239.0B V Y11.8B Loss," ran at 0454 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Marubeni Corp. said Monday its group net profit for the October-December quarter rose 6.9% from a year earlier, helped by stronger earnings from its energy, chemicals and metals businesses. ("Japan's Marubeni Oct-Dec Grp Net Profit Y10.6B Vs Y9.9B," ran at 0552 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Sumitomo Heavy Industries Ltd. said Monday lower costs combined with stronger earnings from its heavy machinery and environmental businesses helped lift its profit for the April-December period. ("Sumitomo Heavy Apr-Dec Grp Net Profit Y15.7B Vs Y8.3B," ran at 0753 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Olympus Corp. Monday reported a sharp decline in its April-December profits, as its profitability continued to suffer from a fall in the prices of digital still cameras as well as an increase in research and development costs. ("Japan Olympus Apr-Dec Group Net Profit Y10.4B Vs Y27.8B," first take ran at 0717 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: NTT Data Corp. said Monday its group net profit for the April-December period grew 17% to Y16.94 billion from Y14.54 billion in the same period a year ago, when a large restructuring-related cost squeezed its profitability. ("Japan's NTT Data Apr-Dec Grp Net Pft Y16.9B Vs Y14.5B," ran at 0650 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Fuji Photo Film Co. Monday reported weak earnings results for the October-December quarter due to higher raw materials costs, reform-related expenses and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  losses. ("Fuji Photo Oct-Dec Group Net Profit Y20.8B Vs Y30.4B," first take ran at 0701 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japan's Omron Corp. said Monday its profits increased on year in the April-December period due to strong domestic and overseas sales of automatic control equipment. ("Japan's Omron Apr-Dec Group Net Profit Y24.8B Vs Y17.1B," first take ran at 0656 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Shiseido Co. said Monday that its group operating profit for the nine months ended December fell on year, hurt by expenses related to sales promotions to shore up its domestic cosmetics \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . ("Japan's Shiseido Apr-Dec Grp Net Profit Y13.7B Vs Y11.8B," first take ran at 0712 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Central Japan Railway Co. said Monday that its group net profit rose 28% in the nine months ended December from a year earlier as it booked a capital gain from the sale of its stake in the Japanese subsidiary of Vodafone Group Plc. ("Japan's JR Tokai Apr-Dec Grp Net Profit Y89.3B Vs Y69.9B," first take ran at 0726 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: UTAC, a small semiconductor tester, will report full-year earnings. (Story to run after 0900 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 ENERGY/COMMODITIES  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japan imported 2.1% less crude oil in 2004 than it did in 2003, despite the country's gasoline demand hitting an all-time high, and oil imports seem set to continue falling. ("=UPDATE: Japan's 2004 Oil Imports Down; Long-Term Trend," ran at 0754 GMT; see also "CORRECT:Japan Re-exported 16.14M KL Refined Oil Pdts '04 " at 0918 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 PERTH: Stunned by demands for a huge price rise, annual negotiations between Australian iron ore producers and Japanese steel makers remain deadlocked, analysts said Monday. ("=Iron Ore Price Talks Deadlocked After Tokyo Stalemate," ran at 0836 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Crude oil futures are trading lower Monday morning in electronic trade on the New York Mercantile Exchange, following OPEC's decision Sunday to keep output steady and abandon its price band. ("Nymex Crude -68c At $46.50 After OPEC Holds Output Steady," ran at 0023 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =CHARTING OIL: Front-month Nymex March crude oil futures, down at $46.59 per barrel after OPEC agreed at the weekend to keep output quotas steady, are set to fall further in coming days, the charts indicate. ("=CHARTING OIL: Nymex Crude Double Top Targets $43.90," ran at 0740 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japanese oil refiners expressed concern Monday that crude import costs will remain high in the months ahead in the wake of OPEC's decision Sunday to scrap its $22-$28 a barrel price band for the group's reference basket of crudes. ("Japan Oil Cos Worry Oil Price To Stay High After OPEC Mtg," ran at 0130 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 NEW DELHI: India's state-run petroleum firm Indian Oil Corp. and Oil India Ltd. have been awarded an onshore oil exploration block in Libya, a senior Indian Oil official said Monday. ("Indian Oil, Oil India Get Libyan Oil Exploration Blk-Exec," first take ran at 0734 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japan's Osaka Gas Co. Monday reported its group net profit fell 5.7% on year in the nine-month period ended Dec. 31, with the introduction of new accounting standards resulting in large special losses. ("Japan's Osaka Gas Expects FY Group Net Profit Y46B," first take ran at 0724 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: China Yangtze Power Co. said Monday it expects to report that its net profit for 2004 rose over 90%. ("China Yangtze Power Predicts 90% Jump For 2004 Net Profit," ran at 0005 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: China's Hangzhou Iron & Steel Co. said it expects the country's steel consumption to rise 14.9% this year as China's economy continues to expand. ("Hangzhou Iron: China Steel Consumption To Rise 15% In '05," ran at 0637 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: The government economic development bureau briefs on the future of Hong Kong's electricity market. (Story possible around 1100 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: A strategic petroleum reserve which China plans to start building in August will not be reserved solely for emergencies, as Beijing plans to use it to moderate prices as well, a leading trade publication said, quoting industry officials. ("China To Use Strategic Oil Stks To Moderate Prices-Report," first take ran at 0250 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 CANBERRA: The labor union representing Australian coal miners Monday threatened to strike in the face of legal action by two major mining companies appealing workplace and safety legislation in New South Wales state. ("Australian Coal Miners Threaten Strike Over Legal Action," ran at 0443 GMT) [ 31-01-05 0926GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 62550  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020050131e11v000gr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
