\par\par \b \uc2 Ex-MOF Mizoguchi: Doubt G7 To Change Wording On  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Much  \b0\par\par\uc2 637 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Januar 2005\par 07:14\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Group of Seven monetary officials are unlikely to change their reference to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in their communique, which will be issued at the end of their planned meeting in London next month, a former top Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  official said Thursday.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Overall conditions in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market haven't changed much since the G7 last met so no change is likely, said Zembei Mizoguchi.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 "I don't think that conditions have changed to the point where the G7 will feel they have to change their wording," said Mizoguchi, who was a vice finance minister for international affairs at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The G7, in a statement issued after their October meeting in Washington, said that "exchange rates should reflect economic fundamentals. Excess volatility and disorderly movements in exchange rates are undesirable for economic growth."  \par \par \uc2 The group also said that "more flexibility in exchange rates is desirable for major countries or economic areas that lack such flexibility to promote smooth and widespread adjustments in the international financial system, based on market mechanisms." [ 27-01-05 0627GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Mizoguchi said the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reference in the communique is "well-balanced, and takes into consideration the basic thinking of Japan, the U.S. and Europe."  \par \par \uc2 That's why there has been no need to alter the wording since it was first introduced at the G7 meeting in Boca Raton, Florida, last February, he said. The same phrasing was used after both the April and October G7 meetings.  \par \par \uc2 "Last year's (Boca Raton) communique was prepared from the standpoint of how the G7 should deal with the dollar's weakness since autumn of the year before," said Mizoguchi, who retired from the ministry in June and is now president of the Japan Center for International Finance in Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 "This year, there are similar circumstances leading up to the London meeting," he said, referring to the dollar's decline against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  since around October. "There hasn't been much shift in the underlying state of affairs."  \par \par \uc2 Analysts say the communique was widely reused last year given that it contains something for everyone. The reference to the need for exchange rates to reflect economic fundamentals, and the warning that "excess volatility and disorderly movements" are "undesirable for economic growth," allows \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to maintain its policy of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets when it feels moves have been too rough.  \par \par \uc2 Even though Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  has stayed out of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market for the past 10 months, it carried out more than Y15 trillion in yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the first three months of last year under the orders of Mizoguchi.  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the call for more exchange rate flexibility in "major countries or economic areas that lack such flexibility" gives the U.S. and Europe an outlet for their frustration with Asian nations, namely China, that keep their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  weak.  \par \par \uc2 Chinese officials, who have been invited to the Feb. 4-5 G7 meeting in London, have repeatedly promised to consider ways to introduce more flexibility in their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  system, but have yet to set a specific timeframe for any changes.  \par \par \uc2 China currently keeps its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the yuan, effectively pegged to the dollar, allowing it to trade within a thin band of 0.3% on either side of 8.277 to the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Mizoguchi said he believes that more time will be needed for Beijing to make necessary reforms and address structural problems in its domestic economy in order to conduct a drastic overhaul of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime.  \par \par \uc2 "I don't think that drastic changes can be made quickly. I think it will take more time," Mizoguchi said.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Miyako Takebe, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; miyako.takebe@dowjones.com   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Hugh Lawson [ 27-01-05 0822GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020050127e11r0006i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
