\par\par \b \uc2 Japan, Thailand agree on new  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  swap deal.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Yoko Nishikawa  \par \uc2 701 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Januar 2005\par 07:04\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Jan 25 (Reuters) - Japan and Thailand agreed to renew a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap pact, sources said on Tuesday, patching up one of the biggest holes in an Asia-wide network designed to ward off the kind of financial crisis that rocked the region in 1997.  \par \par \uc2 After months of negotiations since Thailand decided in July not to extend a $3 billion, three-year bilateral swap facility, both sides decided to revise the agreement to a two-way pact, the sources added.  \par \par \uc2 "We agreed on a two-way \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap deal," one of the sources, who is involved in the negotiations but declined to be identified, told Reuters.  \par \par \uc2 Japan and Thailand, one of the countries hardest-hit by the 1997 crisis, are due to announce the agreement later on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 The agreement is the latest in a series of initiatives springing from the 1997 crisis that were aimed at supporting a country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  when it comes under speculative attack by providing dollars or other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to aid \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been the key driver behind creating a network of swap agreements, which form the Chiang Mai Initiative (CMI) - a plan launched after a meeting of Asian finance ministers in the Thai city of that name in May 2000.  \par \par \uc2 None of the credit lines have been tapped, however, and many analysts regard the CMI as a symbolic tool to enhance regional financial integration and reduce over the long term the influence of Washington and Western-run financial institutions.  \par \par \uc2 The renewed Japan-Thai agreement makes the pact a two-way street, rather than a one-way swap that gives the impression of poor countries being subservient to rich ones. The change reflects Bangkok's recent confidence over its recovery and willingness to graduate from developing country status.  \par \par \uc2 Last July, Thailand's finance ministry had wanted to renew the deal but Prime Minister Thaksin Shinawatra's cabinet voted against it, arguing that Thailand's external reserves have become sufficient.  \par \par \uc2 TWO-WAY STREET  \par \par \uc2 Under the new agreement, Japan would swap up to $3 billion for Thai baht in case of any balance of payment problems or short-term liquidity shortages in Thailand, the same framework as the old pact.  \par \par \uc2 What is new is that Thailand will also commit to swap up to $3 billion for yen in case Japan faces similar problems, making the facility $6 billion in total.  \par \par \uc2 But the latter scenario is considered unlikely as Tokyo holds the world's largest external reserves, totalling over $844 billion. Thailand's foreign reserves stand at around $49 billion.  \par \par \uc2 Japan has been a key creditor in the Asia-wide swap network and its pacts under the CMI are mostly one-way swaps, except for a two-way yen-yuan facility with China.  \par \par \uc2 With the new facility between Japan and Thailand, the CMI, which embraces the 10 members of the Association of Southeast Asian Nations (ASEAN) plus Japan, South Korea and China, has 16 swap lines totalling $37.5 billion.  \par \par \uc2 China and Thailand had a $2 billion swap pact which expired in December. Another source close to the CMI said both countries have not opened negotiations to renew the deal.  \par \par \uc2 As economies in crisis-hit Asian nations have recovered over the past years, however, authorities in the region have faced difficulties in maintaining the momentum for advancing the CMI.  \par \par \uc2 In 2004, no new bilateral swap deal was signed under the CMI.  \par \par \uc2 Officials are currently reviewing the CMI and hope to come up with plans to make it more effective in May, when finance ministers from the ASEAN+3 nations meet on the sidelines of an annual meeting of the Asian Development Bank in Turkey.  \par \par \uc2 Ideas to strengthen the CMI include expanding it, reviewing interest rates on swaps and introducing a system under which one country acts as a coordinator to help activate several bilateral swap lines when a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is under speculative attack.  \par \par \uc2 Also under review is the link between the CMI and the International Monetary Fund, which limits the amount of a swap to 10 percent at any one time. Any amount above that must come in tandem with IMF-mandated economic reforms.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050125e11p006ss\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
