\par\par \b \uc2 DJ INTERVIEW: Dollar To Fall To Y90; Shouldn't Worry MOF  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Stanley White  \par \uc2 526 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Januar 2005\par 21:05\par \uc2 OsterDowJones Commodity Wire\par \uc2 OSTDJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2005, Comtex News Network.  All Rights Reserved.  Standard Attribution Statement: News Provided by COMTEX.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Jan 25, 2005 (ODC via COMTEX) --  \par \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \par \uc2 The dollar will fall to Y90 this year as concerns about U.S. current account and budget deficits return to haunt the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, but Japan should welcome a stronger yen as long as it's gradual, an academic and expert on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Tuesday. Japan should also diversify its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves away from the dollar, but in a slow and controlled fashion so as not to roil financial markets, according to Genki Fujii, visiting professor of international relations at Takushoku University. "Japan has a phobia about a strong yen," Fujii told Dow Jones Newswires in an interview. "But a gradually stronger yen is a healthy thing that will expand domestic consumption by absorbing more U.S. and European imports." Stronger consumer spending, which accounts for over half of Japan's gross domestic product, would filter to other parts of the economy, said Fujii, author of several books about the yen and Japanese economic policy. A strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would also help keep oil and other raw materials prices low, he said. Japan has over $700 billion in U.S. Treasurys in its reserves, making it the largest holder of U.S. government debt in the world. But Japan should reduce the risks of being overexposed to one \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  with a slow and controlled move away from the dollar, Fujii said. A stronger yen, combined with a policy of accumulating less dollars, would also shield Japan from political criticism that Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  need to strengthen more versus the dollar to trim the U.S. current account deficit.  \par \par \uc2 Few Friends Of Strong Yen  \par \par \uc2 Fujii's views are unlikely to win supporters at Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Both Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki and Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Hiroshi Watanabe, who heads \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, have rarely passed up the chance to issue verbal warnings against the yen's rise in recent months. Many players also think the MOF stands ready to act even though it hasn't meddled in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets since ending a record Y35 trillion \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaign last year in March. A stronger yen reduces \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's competitiveness by making its exports more expensive overseas, and many have argued that this threat is too much to bear. However, if the yen's strength is the result of growing confidence in the Japanese economy, the government should welcome this, Fujii said. The MOF has also balked at the mere suggestion that it would reduce the amount of dollars in its foreign reserves. But many central bankers are already thinking along the same lines as Fujii, according to a survey conducted by Central Banking Publications released Monday. Managers of foreign reserves at central banks are more actively diversifying their holdings by \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the survey showed. Over half of the respondents also said euro-zone markets are now as attractive as those in the U.S.  \par \par \uc2 -By Stanley White, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; stanley.white@dowjones.com -Edited by Chris Gallagher  \par \par \uc2 Copyright 2004 Dow Jones. All rights reserved.  \par \par \uc2 Dokument OSTDJ00020050125e11p0020q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
