\par\par \b \uc2 Japan, euro zone draw  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  battle lines ahead of G7.   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Jamie McGeever   \par \uc2 722 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Januar 2005\par 17:56\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Jan 24 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  issues are likely to be high on the agenda of the upcoming Group of Seven meeting, but the dollar may not be the most important focus for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.   \par \par \uc2 A recent increase in rhetoric from euro zone and Japanese officials suggests they're entrenching their positions as the meeting of the world's most powerful finance ministers and central bankers approaches.   \par \par \uc2 Europe wants Asian countries to allow their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  - mainly China's yuan, but also the yen - to appreciate more against the dollar and take some of the burden of general dollar weakness off the euro.   \par \par \uc2 Domestic demand in the euro zone and Japan is still weak, and exports are the lifeline of economic growth for both regions. But strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  crimp a nation's exports.   \par \par \uc2 "While the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  focus in the run-up to G7 meetings normally focuses on the dollar, euro/yen has garnered attention in recent weeks," said Robert Sinche, head of global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at Bank of America in New York.   \par \par \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Jean-Claude Trichet and Chief Economist Otmar Issing have explicitly urged the Asian countries to allow their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to rise, "and the G7 meeting will be used to the fullest for this purpose," BNP Paribas \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts wrote last week.   \par \par \uc2 But Japanese officials, aware that a stronger yen could derail the country's economic recovery and its five-year battle against deflation, see things rather differently.   \par \par \uc2 "Right now, they don't have any commonality of interest," said Adam Posen, senior fellow at the International Institute for Economics in Washington, D.C.   \par \par \uc2 Noting the euro's sharp slide against the yen this year, albeit from a record high at the end of December, Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki was quoted last week as saying euro/yen "needs to be watched fairly carefully."   \par \par \uc2 Indeed, the euro's collapse from its all-time high of 141.61 yen on Dec. 30 is "the single most important development in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market since the start of the year," according to David Woo, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Barclays Capital in London.   \par \par \uc2 CHINA STILL HOLDS THE KEY   \par \par \uc2 The dollar's decline over the past three years has been broad but uneven. The euro has appreciated about 50 percent in that time, while the yen is up 30 percent against the dollar. The euro has risen by 15 percent against the yen, too.   \par \par \uc2 Much of the reason behind the relative lack of yen appreciation is \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's mammoth market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the 15 months to last March, when it bought an unprecedented 35 trillion yen worth of dollars to keep the yen down.   \par \par \uc2 Largely at the insistence of Europe and the United States, recent G7 communiques have called for greater flexibility in global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. The most recent statements specifically targeted "major countries or economic areas that lack such flexibility."   \par \par \uc2 This is a thinly veiled reference to China, which effectively fixes its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the dollar. An official delegation from Beijing will be invited to the Feb. 4-5 meeting in an observer capacity, as was the case at the most recent G7 meeting in Washington in October last year.   \par \par \uc2 Beijing has pledged to move to a fully flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime but has not said when it will take the first step in loosening the yuan's decade-old peg to the dollar.   \par \par \uc2 As long as this remains the case, Asia's export-reliant countries are reluctant to let their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciate.   \par \par \uc2 Daniel Katzive, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at UBS in Stanford, Connecticut, says the G7's call for greater Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility may well fall on deaf ears.   \par \par \uc2 "In the absence of a willingness of Asian policy-makers to significantly alter their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  mechanisms and a lack of enthusiasm from the U.S. policy-makers for seeking to reverse or halt the dollar's weakness, we do not expect this G7 to meaningfully alter the landscape," he wrote last week.   \par \par \uc2 Reflecting this view, UBS initiated a long euro/yen position on Monday, a bet the euro will rise against the yen.   \par \par \uc2 "We believe that short dollar/Asia positions are exposed heading into a G7 meeting that will yield little in the way of commitment to Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation," it said.    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050124e11o00ig8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
