\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  keeps  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  up sleeve as scare tactic.   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Eric Burroughs   \par \uc2 848 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Januar 2005\par 07:32\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Jan 21 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets if the yen threatens nine-year highs against the dollar and any rally accelerates sharply, but any new dollar-buying campaign will probably be limited to scaring speculators.   \par \par \uc2 Tokyo could point to the Group of Seven's warning against excess exchange rate volatility to justify stepping in to buy bundles of dollars if the yen jumped aggressively and threatened peaks since 1995 around 101.25 yen , analysts say.   \par \par \uc2 But Japan is unlikely to defend a specific line in the sand for the dollar/yen rate or come anywhere near the massive 35 trillion yen ($338.6 billion) it spent in the 15 months through March 2004, mainly because the economy and companies are in stronger shape and can better with gradual yen appreciation.   \par \par \uc2 If the yen races higher alone against the euro and dollar rather than rising in line with any broad-based dollar weakness, analysts believe \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will feel warranted in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  - especially if the move is seen as being driven by speculators.   \par \par \uc2 "The key point is the speed of yen appreciation," said Osamu Takashima, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi in Tokyo. "Our feeling is the Bank of Japan and \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  don't feel they have to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if yen appreciation is slow."   \par \par \uc2 The yen jumped against both the dollar and euro at the start of the year as speculators bought the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a way of betting on Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation if China widens the yuan's tight peg to the dollar and lets it float higher.   \par \par \uc2 But pressure has already come off the yen. The dollar climbed to around 103.50 yen on Friday after hitting a five-year low of 101.67 on Monday.   \par \par \uc2 The yen has also fallen from a four-month high against the euro as speculators have cut positions before meetings of the Federal Reserve and G7 finance ministers in early February.   \par \par \uc2 READY TO ACT   \par \par \uc2 As always, Japanese officials are keeping an eye on exchange rates and signalled their readiness repeatedly this week.   \par \par \uc2 Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki said on Friday exchange rates should reflect economic fundamentals and warned Japan will "take appropriate action if needed," while on Tuesday a source at the Finance Ministry told Reuters more bluntly that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was prepared to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  alone.   \par \par \uc2 Investment bank UBS this week raised the possibility that Tokyo had indeed already been covertly dabbling in the market.   \par \par \uc2 "There is some speculation that Japanese authorities have been using less direct methods to influence the yen via foreign bond purchases by quasi-government agencies," UBS \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Ashley Davies said in a note to clients.   \par \par \uc2 Davies said there was no direct evidence that this was the case but noted other Asian central banks have picked up the pace of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent months to cushion the dollar's fall.   \par \par \uc2 "We think that the risks of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the next few weeks are very high and, should the BOJ finally re-emerge in the markets, it will likely be with a bang," he wrote.   \par \par \uc2 Politically, though, it would be awkward for Tokyo to justify \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to hold down the yen at a time when the G7 is quietly cajoling Beijing to let the yuan rise.   \par \par \uc2 If China does in fact let the yuan appreciate, the yen is likely to strengthen along with other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 "Under such circumstances, it is difficult for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to maintain aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to manipulate dollar/yen," said \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists at JPMorgan Chase in a note to clients.   \par \par \uc2 ECONOMY BETTER   \par \par \uc2 Japan's economy and its corporations are also much healthier than they were during the massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaign, so they could cope better with a rise in the yen's value.   \par \par \uc2 Companies, in particular, are enjoying solid profits and cash flow, while large exporters are increasingly diversified in their exposure to exchange rates because their manufacturing \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  are spread around the world.   \par \par \uc2 Japan's economy is still trapped in deflation - a crucial argument in the past for resisting yen appreciation - but the picture is gradually brightening.   \par \par \uc2 Core consumer prices fell 0.2 percent in the 12 months to November. In mid-2002, they were falling at a 0.9 percent pace.   \par \par \uc2 Japan's economic expansion is expected to be sustained this year, even though growth has slowed in the past six months.   \par \par \uc2 For those reasons and others, JPMorgan strategists Tohru Sasaki and Junya Tanase have been much more sceptical about the likelihood of a resumption of official \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 In a report this week, the Tokyo-based strategists said Japanese exporters were likely to set their hedges for the 2005 fiscal year and plan for a dollar/yen rate of around 100 to 102 yen, turning them into yen buyers soon.   \par \par \uc2 Thus they believe \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unlikely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  around the 100 yen level unless it is a joint initiative with other G7 members or unless dollar/yen collapses 2-3 yen in one day.   \par \par \uc2 "We believe the risk of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has significantly receded," Sasaki and Tanase said.    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050121e11l006n1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
