\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: Japan Policy-Makers Worry  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Could Hurt Econ  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Takashi Nakamichi  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 876 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Januar 2005\par 10:40\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par      TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japan's government Wednesday warned for the first time in 10 months in its monthly economic report that the yen's recent gains could hurt the economy, while policy-makers chorused their concerns about the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's rise.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan Governor Toshihiko Fukui said that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market has been unstable and he would watch closely for any impact on the economy.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 He told a regular news conference that Group of Seven finance ministers and central bankers at a meeting Feb. 4-5 in London would "more strongly confirm" their view that disorderly moves aren't welcome and exchange rates should reflect fundamentals.  \par \par \uc2 The government's report and comments from policy-makers follow gains in the yen against the dollar and other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  this week. The dollar touched a five-year low of Y101.67 Monday, while the euro Wednesday fell to a four-month low of Y132.99.  \par \par \uc2 Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki wouldn't comment on recent \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates but threatened to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in markets to stop the yen's rise. "We need to monitor the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market closely, and are ready to take proper action against excessive moves," he said.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  hasn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets since it ended in March last year a 15-month campaign to cap the yen.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par      Yen Has Gained Against Dollar, Euro \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A stronger yen would further depress already sluggish exports and hurt industrial production.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With that possibility in mind, both the government and Bank of Japan left unchanged their cautious assessment of the economy in monthly reports, and the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's policy board said in reviewing forecasts it issued last October that economic activity is slightly weaker than it had expected.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The economic recovery has been moderating recently, with some parts of the economy showing weakness," the Cabinet Office said in its report.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While the economic recovery is expected to progress firmly in line with a global recovery, "there's a need to be mindful of such things as inventory adjustments in information-related sectors and trends in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates and crude oil prices."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It was the first time since April last year that the government report explicitly mentioned \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates as a risk factor.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We need to monitor \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rate movements carefully," a Cabinet Office official briefing reporters on the economic report said. "Since January, the yen has appreciated not only against the dollar but also against the euro. Even when viewed through the broad picture of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates, the yen is strong."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par      Fukui Expects G7 To Warn On \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 The yen's gains have in part been prompted by expectations that European and U.S. officials might increase pressure on Asian countries to strengthen their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at the G7 meeting to ease global trade imbalances.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Fukui said that those at the meeting will "no doubt" discuss global imbalances, likely referring to the U.S.' large current account and budget deficits.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Recent \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  moves have been somewhat unstable," he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Fukui made the comments at a press conference after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's board voted unanimously to keep monetary policy steady in Japan.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In its monthly report, the BOJ reiterated that "Japan's economy continues in a recovery trend, although there seem to be somewhat weak movements mainly in production."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The BOJ last October forecast that real gross domestic product would grow by 3.6% this fiscal year ending March 31 and 2.5% next fiscal year. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  didn't issue new forecasts for GDP but did note a change in the method of calculating GDP introduced by the government last November. The change led to a cut in previous GDP estimates.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The BOJ said that taking into account the new method, policy board members' forecasts for real GDP growth could be translated to around 2.5% for this fiscal year and 1.5% for next fiscal year.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  last October also forecast that the core CPI would fall by 0.2% this fiscal year but rise 0.1% next fiscal year, which would satisfy one of the BOJ's conditions for ending its quantitative easing.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Consumer prices "are also expected to be basically in line" with last October's forecasts. But due to a a reduction in telephone charges, consumer prices "may deviate slightly below" the BOJ's forecasts, it said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The government repeated its view that exports, production and consumption are either weak or growing at a slower pace.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It maintained its assessment that industrial production "is weakening." In addition to moderating overseas demand for Japan-made goods, Japanese manufacturers' increased efforts to reduce inventories of digital parts and devices have weighed on output, it said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Exports are still weakening while imports are "leveling off," the government said. But it downgraded its assessment of Japan's surplus of trade in goods and services, saying the surplus is moderately decreasing instead of saying it is "flat."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Takashi Nakamichi, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com (Hiroshi Inoue, Leika Kihara, Takeshi Takeuchi and Asako Tanabe contributed to this article.)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Hugh Lawson [ 19-01-05 0940GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020050119e11j000dk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
