\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  VIEW: Eye On  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2 , But  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2 's No Sure Bet  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,102 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Januar 2005\par 22:10\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Laurence Norman \par    A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Just as political writers had a field day last month playing guess the second term Bush cabinet, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market watchers are now wallowing in one of their own favorite games: guess when Japanese authorities will \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to weaken the yen.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 The yen's rise versus the euro and the dollar since the start of the year, fueled by a sense that Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  will outperform the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2005, has focused attention back on possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . So has the persistent speculation that China might be preparing to revalue the yuan.  \par \par \uc2 Yet many analysts and traders say that while Japanese authorities may seem more anxious than a few weeks ago, there are still plenty of reasons to doubt \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is imminent.  \par \par \uc2 The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in late 2003 and early 2004 on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The bank bought almost Y15 trillion worth of dollars in the first quarter of 2004 to stem the yen's rise. It hasn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  since then, even while most other Asian central banks have been lapping up dollars and as the yen made significant gains in the fourth quarter.  \par \par \uc2 In December 2004, the yen lagged the euro's sharp rise against the dollar. The result was that while the euro set a run of record highs versus the dollar, it also hit an all-time high versus the yen on Dec. 29 at Y141.64.  \par \par \uc2 Yet at the start of 2005, the market mood switched. Profit taking on long euro/short yen positions sent the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  snapping higher and by last Friday, it had hit a fresh five-year high versus the dollar and four-month high versus the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Y132.99.  \par \par \uc2 It's not surprising then that the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  chorus is becoming more audible again. As the yen rose, Japanese officials began to chime in on the dangers of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength. There were the usual warnings that officials were closely watching the market from Japanese Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki and that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was very much an option.  \par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, BOJ officials gave the market extra reason to fear they would step in. In an interim review of October's economic outlook report, the BOJ said "Japan's economy has deviated slightly below the outlook." It also said inflation may temporarily fall below previously predicted levels - raising the specter of renewed deflation. Since export growth has been one of the economy's few growth sectors, a tougher outlook could make Japanese authorities more eager to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par      China A Large Influence \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Fresh speculation that China may soon revalue its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has placed further upside pressure on the yen. Since it's hard for hedge funds and large banks to make bets directly on the yuan because of capital controls, the yen is used by some as a proxy, because most expect a Chinese revaluation to spark a stronger yen.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In research reports Wednesday, Ashley Davies, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at UBS in Singapore, said there were "growing concerns in the market about the prospects of a BOJ \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  with 101.50 rumored to be a key level to watch." Davies suggested that this time around, Asian central banks may start buying euro, not dollars, during \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Davies also suggested Japanese authorities may have remained active in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets beyond last March. He said while the BOJ had stood back, there are indications of "unofficial \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 " by "quasi-government agencies" such as trust banks and pension funds, which were under the "influence" of the government. This, Davies said, adds to the chance that the BOJ will \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  if the yen starts strengthening again.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Yet many analysts are less certain, particularly because Japan has shown itself as able to stomach a lot more yen strength already.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "There are always \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rumors," said Steve Saywell, chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at Citigroup in London. "But we haven't seen any reason to believe the risk is heightened, or that we've entered some danger zone.".  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Saywell also played down the notion that the level of the euro versus the yen has gained new importance for Japanese policymakers as they contemplate \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The real issue is still dollar-yen," said Saywell, who said that the next big target for the dollar is the Y101.30 level. A breach of that would trigger a massive number of options and dollar losses would likely accelerate toward the Y100 level. At that point, he says, Japanese officials would be likely to step in to slow the pace of the yen's ascent, rather than to try singlehandedly to turn the rally around.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Koichi Kubo, Treasurer at Mizuho Corporate Bank, in New York agreed that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is unlikely unless the dollar falls to Y100, as long as the rising yen doesn't hit Japanese stocks and bond prices or create "deflationary pressures."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Kubo noted that Japanese companies don't seem too worried by the yen's rise since most firms haven't been repatriating dollar holdings from abroad to prevent the falling dollar reducing the value of those assets. Pressure from exporters was seen as a key factor in persuading \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the past.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par      G7 Complicating Factor \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Economists have given a host of reasons why Japanese companies and officials may be less worried by the stronger yen, including increased Japanese production in China, the higher oil price and ongoing confidence that domestic demand will recover this year. They have also noted that the BOJ and some government officials are reluctant to repeat last year's messy, massive daily \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Others say that with the Group of Seven industrialized nations meeting due Feb 4-5, Japanese authorities will be reluctant to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and risk being accused of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  manipulation.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While it is "clear" that Japanese authorities are monitoring the markets closely and are "very nervous" about yen strength, they will "probably" do nothing until after the G7 meeting, said Toshiyuki Suzuki, senior economist at UFJ Bank in New York.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Once that is out the way, if the yen is still under upward pressure - and especially if speculation about a China revaluation has not died down - \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may well be a more realistic option.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Laurence Norman covers \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for Dow Jones Newswires in New York. He has previously covered the Argentine economy for Dow Jones in Buenos Aires.)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Laurence Norman, Dow Jones Newswires, 1-201-938-2096; laurence.norman@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Steven Vames contributed to this article.) [ 01-19-05 1610ET ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020050119e11j0005l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
