\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar Rises Vs Euro In Morning Trading; Yen Up Vs Euro  \b0\par\par\uc2 945 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Januar 2005\par 17:13\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--The dollar headed sharply higher versus the euro in New York trading Wednesday, while the yen bounced back from overnight losses against the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The recovery left the dollar little changed from its levels of late New York trading Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 Renewed speculation over if and when the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the yen's rise lent support to the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said traders. Any such move would likely not only lift the dollar versus the yen, but also probably push the yen down versus the euro.  \par \par \uc2 Late morning, the euro was fetching $1.3022, off its session high of $1.3318 but little changed from $1.3023 late Tuesday. The dollar was at Y102.54, off its session low of Y102.66 and up from Y102.31. Against the Swiss franc, the dollar was at CHF1.1831 from CHF1.1853. The pound was at $1.8760 from $1.8659.  \par \par \uc2 The euro was at Y133.55, down from its session high of Y134.39, but higher than Y133.22 late Tuesday. In overnight Asian trading, the euro had dipped to Y132.99 versus the yen, before lifting sharply. That was the strongest the yen had been versus the euro since early September. The euro hit a record high versus the yen on Dec. 29 at Y141.64.  \par \par \uc2 The yen fell back overnight after comments from Bank of Japan Governor Toshihiko Fukui in which he said "recent \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves have been somewhat unstable," and expressed concern about a recent rise in the yen against the dollar and the euro. That suggested the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the yen's gain.  \par \par \uc2 A report on the December U.S. consumer price index, which arrived generally in line with expectations, ultimately had little effect on the market. The dollar caught a bid directly following the release of report early in New York trading [ 01-19-05 1113ET ]  \par \par \uc2 There appears to be considerable concern over \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In an overnight research report, UBS strategists said "the market is getting wary of BoJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with some in the market talking about Y101.50 as a key level to watch."  \par \par \uc2 UBS said that given the the high dollar holdings of Asian central banks in general, the region's officials may start buying the euro, instead of the dollar, to stem their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ' rise. That could spark considerable concern among the Europeans, who have long warned that the strong euro is hurting their economy.  \par \par \uc2 Overnight, South Korean officials also warned they would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the Korean won kept rising. The won reached a seven-year high overnight.  \par \par \uc2 However, there are plenty of analysts who feel the BoJ's threshold is not so close.  \par \par \uc2 Koichi Kubo, Treasurer at Mizuho Corporate Bank, said he continues to believe that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not likely before the dollar falls to at least Y100, as long as the rising yen does not hit the Japanese stock market and bond yields or create "deflationary pressures."  \par \par \uc2 He said that a sign of the lack of concern among Japanese companies about the yen's rise is the fact that most firms have not been repatriating dollars holdings from abroad to prevent the falling dollar reducing the value of those assets. Pressure from exporters was seen as a key factor in persuading the BoJ to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the past.  \par \par \uc2 Other analysts noted that with the Group of Seven industrialized nations meeting around the corner, the Japanese may not want to anger the U.S. and Europe by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 While the dollar was partly lifted by the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  talk, it was also helped by the latest in a series of supportive data, said Kristjan Kasikov, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Calyon in London.  \par \par \uc2 "With the strong data releases on jobless claims today and portfolio flows yesterday, we do have some supportive factors for the dollar," Kasikov said.  \par \par \uc2 A report Wednesday said initial jobless claims fell by 48,000 to 319,000 in the week that ended Jan. 15, the biggest decline since the week of Dec. 8, 2001. Wall Street expected a much smaller 22,000 decline in claims.  \par \par \uc2 Treasury data Tuesday showed an $81 billion inflow in November, which was more than enough to cover the $60.3 billion trade deficit registered that month. But some analysts played down the inflows, saying they were due to an unsustainable post-election resurgence in foreign appetite for U.S. equities.  \par \par \uc2 Kasikov added that comments from officials have also helped the dollar reverse from its session lows. In particular, Fed Governor Ben Bernanke said that low rates are not causing excessive risk taking, and that Fed rate increases are not posing a significant threat to U.S. growth.  \par \par \uc2 Additionally, markets were reminded of the dollar-supportive interest rate environment, where the U.S. is raising rates while Europe and Japan are not.  \par \par \uc2 German lawmakers recounted comments from European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governing Council member Axel Weber, who said Wednesday he sees no reason for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to change interest rates. Weber, who is also the president of the Deutsche Bundesbank, made the comments during a meeting with a committee of the German parliament.  \par \par \uc2 The lawmakers said that Weber also stated he doesn't expect a big change in the euro-dollar exchange rate as long as the U.S. current account deficit remains.  \par \par \uc2 -By Laurence Norman, Dow Jones Newswires, 201-938-2096; laurence.norman@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -By Steven Vames, Dow Jones Newswires, 201-938-5063; steven.vames@dowjones.com [ 01-19-05 1134ET ]  \par \par \uc2 1074 1863 28038 4164  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020050119e11j0004d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
