\par\par \b \uc2 DJ WSJ(1/20) Economic Impact Of Yen's Gains Worries Japanese  \b0\par\par\uc2 611 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Januar 2005\par 21:05\par \uc2 OsterDowJones Commodity Wire\par \uc2 OSTDJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2005, Comtex News Network.  All Rights Reserved.  Standard Attribution Statement: News Provided by COMTEX.  \par \par \uc2 Jan 19, 2005 (ODC via COMTEX) --  \par \par \uc2 (From THE WALL STREET JOURNAL)  \par \par \uc2 By Takashi Nakamichi and Ginny Parker TOKYO -- Japan's government warned in its monthly economic report that the yen's recent gains could hurt the economy, while policy makers voiced their concerns about the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise. Bank of Japan governor Toshihiko Fukui said the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market has been unstable and that he would watch closely for any impact on the economy. The comments followed gains in the yen against the dollar and other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier in the week. The dollar touched a five-year low of 101.67 yen Monday, while the euro early yesterday fell to a four-month low of about 133 yen. The euro rose against the yen in global trading later yesterday, after Mr. Fukui's comments were reported. In morning trading in New York, the euro was quoted at about 133.70 yen. The dollar was at 102.18 yen. The yen's gains have been prompted partly by expectations that European and U.S. officials at meetings next month of the Group of Seven industrialize nations might increase pressure on Asian countries to strengthen their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to ease global trade imbalances. Mr. Fukui said that those at the meeting will "no doubt" discuss global imbalances, likely referring to the U.S.'s large current-account and budget deficits. Japanese Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki threatened to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in markets to stop the yen's rise. "We need to monitor the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market closely, and are ready to take proper action against excessive moves," he said. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  hasn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets since it ended last March a 15-month campaign to cap the yen. A stronger yen would further depress already sluggish exports and hurt industrial production. With that possibility in mind, both the government and Bank of Japan left unchanged their cautious assessment of the economy in monthly reports, and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy board said in reviewing forecasts it issued in October that economic activity is slightly weaker than it had expected. It was the first time since April of last year that the government report explicitly mentioned \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates as a risk factor. Companies doing business in Japan yesterday offered a contrasting sign of confidence in the strength of the country's economic recovery. In a quarterly survey global recruitment firm Hudson Global Resources found that companies were more eager to hire employees than they have been in nearly three years. The poll of 540 companies in Japan, most of them foreign, found that 50% plan to hire new permanent employees in the January to March period, up from 42% that said they would do so in the previous quarter. It was the first time since the 2002 July-September quarter that at least half the respondents said they planned to add staff. "Japan's economy has obviously recovered to quite an extent and companies are catching up to that," said Stephen Romaine, Hudson's Japan country manager. "They're focused on the infrastructure and making sure it's able to handle the increased business." A rise in hiring would bode well for Japan's two-year-old economic recovery, which is starting to slow as personal consumption remains weak despite strong corporate spending. Concerns about job security and unemployment have been a key reason consumers have refused to loosen their purse strings. Hudson found that most companies polled were optimistic about future profits, with 64% expecting excellent or good performance over the next six months.  \par \par \uc2 Copyright 2004 Dow Jones. All rights reserved.  \par \par \uc2 Dokument OSTDJ00020050120e11j0014l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
