\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Dollar Plummets After Trade Data Widens -2-  \b0\par\par\uc2 473 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Januar 2005\par 16:09\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2005, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 Lara Rhame, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Credit Suisse First Boston said the deficit number 'is probably going to spark a severe dollar sell-off.' She said the downward pressure will likely continue at least until the Group Of Seven leading industrial nations meeting at the beginning of February.  \par \par \uc2 She said the dollar could hit new record lows versus the euro by the G7 meeting. That would mean the euro climbing above $1.3660.  \par \par \uc2 Rhame said the trade number also 'means that ahead of the G7 meeting, we are going to see a severe intensification of the rhetoric on global imbalances.' That's despite the fact that the U.S. deficit with China actually narrowed slightly in November from the previous month.  \par \par \uc2 That will likely include a renewed focus on China's effective \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg with the dollar - something that many analysts blame for the U.S. running a huge bilateral trade deficit with China. Both European and U.S. officials have been pressing the Chinese on the issue. China has been invited to attend the G7 meeting, just as it was at the last meeting in October 2004.  \par \par \uc2 In a precursor to that heightened rhetoric, European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief economist Otmar Issing on Tuesday criticized Asian countries, and China in particular, for not contributing enough to the reduction of global imbalances stemming from the large U.S. current account deficit.  \par \par \uc2 'China is still putting off its contribution, but it will no doubt at some stage act,' he said, speaking at a conference about liquidity organized by the Swiss National Bank.  \par \par \uc2 And late Tuesday, U.S. Commerce Secretary Don Evans also called on the Chinese to move to a flexible exchange rate during a visit to Beijing.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow speaks in New York at 9.30 a.m. EST.  \par \par \uc2 At 8:58 a.m. EST, the yen strengthened to Y102.21 versus the dollar, hitting its highest level since early December when it tapped a 4 1/2-year high at Y101.86.  \par \par \uc2 That may have prompted Japanese Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki to say that the government should closely monitor the exchange rate movement of the yen against the U.S. dollar.  \par \par \uc2 'I believe we must closely monitor the yen-dollar exchange rate,' Tanigaki told reporters during a trip to New Delhi in his latest hint that authorities in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  are prepared to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 While the yen lagged the euro's gains in late December, there is a growing sense that this year the Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are better placed to make gains versus the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 That is particularly true if China does make some change to its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg, since many analysts think Japan, South Korea and other Asian countries' tolerance for stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  will increase if the yuan also appreciates.  \par \par \uc2 - -   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020050112e11c008n7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
