\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  VIEW: Asia Seen Picking Up More Of Dollar's Fall  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,103 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Januar 2005\par 17:30\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Laurence Norman and Steven Vames \par    A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--The euro may be passing the baton to the yen and other Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 After three years during which the European common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  shouldered the burden of the dollar's adjustment, gaining about 47% against the buck compared with a 22% gain in the yen, many in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets believe this is the year of Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength. That scenario depends to a large extent however, on China's intentions.  \par \par \uc2 The world hasn't suddenly changed overnight. The U.S. and others in the Group of Seven leading industrialized nations, have long been calling for more \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility in areas that lack them - read China in particular but other Asian countries also. The call was first issued in the G7 finance ministers statement in Dubai in September 2003.  \par \par \uc2 The problem was Asian monetary authorities didn't appear to heed the call. At the time, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily in markets in a bid to stop a strong yen from derailing its nascent recovery. Other Asian central banks followed suit and China showed no signs of moving toward a more flexible exchange rate system.  \par \par \uc2 But 2004 yielded an important lesson: Japan may be prepared to allow the yen to appreciate. An ardent fan of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  hasn't actually sought to directly influence the yen since the end of March. Asian official investors - led by Japan and China - remain large presences in the U.S. fixed income markets, but analysts believe the authorities in Asia are more inclined now to allow their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to edge higher.  \par \par \uc2 Of course, the Chinese yuan looms as a massive influence on just how much things will ultimately change and crucially, when that will happen. If China were to introduce a more flexible rate regime for its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  - which has a de facto peg to the dollar - it could encourage other Asian nations to allow their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to strengthen too, since they will have to depend less on a weak \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to compete with China. That could further broaden the strength of those \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  versus the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 If China doesn't loosen the peg this year, there is more debate about whether gains in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  will outstrip the euro's appreciation versus the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The failure of Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to make bigger gains so far has sparked major complaints by euro-zone officials, who say it's hurting their domestic growth prospects. To make matters worse for European exporters, the euro rose to an all time high of Y141.64 last month, though it has since fallen back.  \par \par \uc2 "Most people feel we need an adjustment of the Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , since they have been bearing a disproportionally smaller load of the dollar's decline," said John McCarthy, director of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at ING Capital Markets in New York.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     Still Subdued \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Any acceleration in relative gains for Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  versus the dollar would mark a departure from recent trends. In the late days of 2004, the euro reached record highs against both the dollar and yen, as speculative trading cumulated in a year-end frenzy. Overall, in the fourth quarter, the euro gained 9% versus the dollar, while the greenback fell 6.8% versus the yen.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Similarly on the way down: from New Year's Eve through the first week of 2005, the dollar gained about 2.6% versus the euro while it advanced just under 1.5% versus the yen and 0.4% versus the won.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In part, the euro's outperformance in late 2004 was a matter of market efficiency and convenience. The heavy speculative trading was above all driven by dollar weakness, rather than anything to do with the euro. Investors - keen to profit from the trend but fearful that positions could get burned in anything other than the most liquid \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , chose the euro as the easiest way to bet against the dollar.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Additionally, the euro's outperformance against the yen stemmed from simple fear of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which left some investors reluctant to push the yen stronger. But now, in addition to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's apparently reduced penchant for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , some other signs suggest Asian countries might be getting more comfortable with stronger \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Asia's commitment to its hard and soft dollar pegs is waning as policymakers perceive the cost of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  - sterilization, excessive reserve concentration in U.S. assets, rising inflation - to outweigh benefits such as export competitiveness," said \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  analysts at JP Morgan, in a 2005 outlook. "The absence of substantive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in recent months despite a regional slowdown suggests policy priorities have shifted."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Of course, the threat of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  manipulation is still there. In recent months, Korea has \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  massively to support the won - and it has made it clear it will not hold back from stepping into the market this year either. Indonesia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has stepped into markets, while the Thai \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  governor said last month authorities will act to keep exports competitive. Neither Malaysia nor Hong Kong looks likely to give up their dollar pegs.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Still, even without a move from China, some analysts believe exchange rate management could be getting more liberal in Asia.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "With continued improvements in the domestic economy and signs that other Asian countries...are allowing appreciation against the dollar, (Japan) may feel more comfortable moving the dollar-yen target range lower," said Andy Bevan, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at Goldman Sachs in London.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bevan says an effective way to take advantage of the strategy might simply be to bet on the yen versus the euro. "Both \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  should continue to rally relative to the dollar in the foreseeable future, but the fundamentals now seem to be in place for a convincing outperformance of the yen relative to the euro," he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meanwhile, in a research report last week, BNP Paribas made a similar argument about Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strength and said taking short positions in the Australian dollar and British pound versus the yen would be a good way to profit from the stronger yen story.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Laurence Norman and Steven Vames cover \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for Dow Jones Newswires in New York. Norman has previously covered the Argentine economy for Dow Jones in Buenos Aires. Vames has also covered equities in Los Angeles and the Treasurys market and fixed income industry in New York.)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Laurence Norman, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-2096; laurence.norman@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Steven Vames; Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-5063; steven.vames@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 01-12-05 1130ET  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020050112e11c00025\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
