\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-BOJ's Fukui cautiously optimistic on Japan economy.   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Natsuko Waki   \par \uc2 422 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Januar 2005\par 18:25\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 BASEL, Switzerland, Jan 9 (Reuters) - Bank of Japan Governor Toshihiko Fukui said on Sunday he was cautiously optimistic about the outlook for Japan's economy but potential irregular market movements, including \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , could pose a risk.   \par \par \uc2 "I think the outlook (for Japan's economy) is not so bad. I am always cautiously optimistic," he told Reuters on the sidelines of a bi-monthly meeting of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors at the Bank for International Settlements.   \par \par \uc2 After robust growth in the first three months of 2004, Japan's economic recovery has been showing signs of slowing as demand for exports moderates.   \par \par \uc2 The economy grew just 0.2 percent in July-September on an annualised basis following a contraction of 0.6 percent in April-June, a reversal from the first three months of 2004 when it grew at an annualised rate of 6.8 percent.   \par \par \uc2 Fukui said one of the downside risks for the world's second-largest economy was how long an IT inventory adjustment would last. "But it is widely said that nothing like the adjustment we saw in 2000-2001 would happen this time," he said.   \par \par \uc2 Fukui, speaking in New York last week, said he was confident about the Japanese economy given rising capital expenditure, shrinking excess capacities of firms as well as falling unemployment and non-performing loans.   \par \par \uc2 Fukui later told reporters the underlying recovery trend of the Japanese economy remained intact.   \par \par \uc2 WATCHING \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  CAREFULLY   \par \par \uc2 A sharp yen appreciation could also damage the recovery of Japan's export-oriented economy. The yen hit a near five-year high of 101.81 per dollar in December, driven by broad dollar weakness, but has since fallen back to around 104.50 yen.   \par \par \uc2 "Including \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 , there is a risk markets overall could move in an irregular manner at any time. So in that regard \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements could be a risk and we are watching them carefully," Fukui said.   \par \par \uc2 With signs of a solid recovery, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  halted its yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in mid-March last year, after selling 35 trillion yen in the five quarters to the end of March 2004.   \par \par \uc2 Japan has said the economy can now withstand a higher yen.   \par \par \uc2 Fukui said the two-day meeting of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors from the G10 and key emerging economies would discuss a wide range of issues including the outlook for the global economy.   \par \par \uc2 The meeting is also attended by heads of commercial banks and financial regulators around the world.    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050109e119008et\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
