\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-BOJ's Fukui says monitoring  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  mkts closely.   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Jamie McGeever   \par \uc2 664 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Januar 2005\par 21:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Jan 6 (Reuters) - Japan is aware that excessive exchange rate market volatility could dent its economic recovery and is monitoring \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets very closely, Bank of Japan Governor Toshihiko Fukui said on Thursday.   \par \par \uc2 Responding to a question on Asian central banks' large \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, Fukui said: "I don't deny that there is some possibility that some developments in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets could throw the Japanese economy off course."   \par \par \uc2 "Therefore we are monitoring \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets very, very closely," he said.   \par \par \uc2 Fukui was participating in a question-and-answer session at the Japan Society, his first public speaking engagement in New York.   \par \par \uc2 He later told reporters, however, that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market moves have up to now been relatively smooth, which would suggest there is no major threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the very near term.   \par \par \uc2 "Up to present, market movements have been orderly, or not so disorderly, fortunately," he said after his speech.   \par \par \uc2 Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , via the BOJ, sold an unprecedented 35 trillion yen in the five quarters through the end of last March to prevent the yen from strengthening too much and damaging the competitiveness of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's exports.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  since March 16, 2004.   \par \par \uc2 The dollar subsequently fell to a near five-year low of 101.81 yen in December last year, but has since rebounded to around 105.00 yen, a level that most analysts say Japanese exporters will be more comfortable with.   \par \par \uc2 The next key level which \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market participants are watching for Japan to buy dollars is around the psychologically important 100-yen-per-dollar barrier.   \par \par \uc2 NO PLANS TO CHANGE MONETARY POLICY   \par \par \uc2 Citing structural reforms and improvements in the domestic economy, Fukui said he is confident Japan can continue to grow.   \par \par \uc2 "As a central banker, I am always cautiously optimistic on the Japanese economy. But I am more optimistic on Japan's economy now than I was when I became BoJ Governor," he said.   \par \par \uc2 Fukui explained that was because capital expenditure is rising, firms' excess capacities are shrinking, unemployment is falling and non-performing loans are being cleaned up.   \par \par \uc2 "Japan's recovery, therefore, is on a much stronger footing" than two previous recoveries in the 1990s, he said.   \par \par \uc2 Japan's economy slowed dramatically from a strong first quarter in 2004 as the year progressed, narrowly avoiding contraction in the third quarter.   \par \par \uc2 Fukui noted that while Japan's recovery was undergoing "a temporary pause," due to slower global growth, he was confident domestic improvements and continued world growth will keep the country's recovery on track.   \par \par \uc2 "As long as the global economy continues to recover, it's unlikely that Japan's economy will fall behind," he said.   \par \par \uc2 On the ongoing battle against deflation, he said market expectations are still "extremely subdued" as to when the consumer price index (CPI) will turn positive.   \par \par \uc2 "It's uncertain when we can expect a positive (core) CPI reading on a sustainable basis," he said. "There is some possibility at the end of fiscal year 2005 it comes up above zero percent, but it's still uncertain."   \par \par \uc2 In this light, there is "no intention to change the BOJ's stance on monetary policy," he said, referring to the bank's "quantitative easing" policy, which floods the banking system with excess liquidity, effectively keeping interest rates at near zero.   \par \par \uc2 Deflation has plagued the Japanese economy over five years, but has eased somewhat in the past year, with consumer prices hovering just below zero.   \par \par \uc2 U.S. imbalances are "worrisome," he said, and cannot go on indefinitely. He added, however, the U.S. can run these longer than most countries because of the efficient allocation of capital in the economy and because foreign capital inflows into the United States are sufficient to fund it.   \par \par \uc2 The wide U.S. current account deficit - a broad measure of the nation's global trade - is a persistent weight on the U.S. dollar.    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050106e11600jwr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
