\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  VIEW: Ten Key Issues For  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Markets In 2005  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Laurence Norman and Steven Vames  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 1,087 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Dezember 2004\par 22:17\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par .   .   \f28 \par \par \uc2 New York (Dow Jones)--A third successive year of declines for the dollar provided the background for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements in 2004, particularly after a head fake early in the year ultimately led to a dollar plummet later on.  \par \par \uc2 The presidential election, oil prices, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's massive first-quarter \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the twin U.S. budget and current account deficits were the main market movers and shakers over the past 12 months.  \par \par \uc2 For 2005, here are 10 key issues that should weigh heavily in the year ahead.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Will China revalue the yuan? \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This was the question that received most attention from analysts and it remains just as key as the new year dawns. A yuan revaluation would have some impact on the massive U.S. trade imbalance with China. More importantly, it would make policy makers in Japan and other Asian countries more relaxed about a falling dollar and shift some of the burden of a weak dollar away from the euro, helping that region's struggling economy.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Will there be unilateral \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ? \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Though broad coordination on policy would be most effective, a lack of agreement could cause some countries officials to go it alone. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  remains the most likely to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , since officials have been steadily warning markets. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to the tune of almost Y15 trillion in the first quarter of 2003 to stem dollar weakness. The ECB has also raised \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as an option, and its clear that the euro-zone economy is feeling the pain of double-edged euro strength versus both dollar and yen. Others see the ECB cutting rates in a bid to boost growth and slow the euro's rise.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    What will happen with the U.S. current account deficit? \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The current account has been the primary factor in the dollar's fourth quarter slide. The deficit now stands at 5.7% of GDP, above the 5% level that many economists - and even the Federal Reserve - has signaled as a danger zone. What happens with the deficit will depend on the behavior of U.S. consumers as well as the willingness of foreigners - especially from Asia - to continue funding the deficit at current interest-rates. All will watch closely to see if the dollar's declines start reducing the deficit.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Will a falling dollar hurt U.S. bond and stock markets? \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Liquidation of U.S. assets becomes a risk as the dollar slides, yet analysts are split on whether this may happen. It could come as foreign central banks or private investors demand higher returns for the risk of holding dollar assets, or it could be prevented if the weak dollar helps lift stocks. There have also been recent signs that U.S. residents could trigger the process by pulling out of local markets and investing in foreign stocks and bonds to gain from the dollar's fall.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Will central banks steer clear of dollars? \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Rapidly growing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves are becoming ever more important for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets, and concern has arisen that Asian and OPEC central banks are buying euros, not dollars, as they pile up reserves. Russia has said this publicly. Nothing could send the dollar crashing more quickly than a dumping of dollar reserves by central banks.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     Will oil continue to affect \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ? \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar's latest bout of declines was in part triggered by oil prices rising above $55.50 a barrel in October, a nominal record. The situation in Iraq, the Mid-East peace process and wider tensions in the region will surely play into oil prices, yet analysts' predictions for 2005 are all over the place. A sharp rise or fall in prices could have big consequences for each of the major global economies, and could affect levels of international tolerance for a weaker dollar.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    How will the U.S. economy perform? \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Paradoxically, recent dollar declines came amid above-trend U.S. growth which outpaced that of the euro zone and Japan. Yet with the current account deficit such a concern, stronger growth has raised the specter of a wider deficit and has at times harmed, not helped, the dollar. Yet some analysts think that strong growth - if it lifts stocks and allows the Fed to keep raising rates - could become more important for the dollar in 2005 if the euro zone and Japanese economies continue to flounder.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Will Chinese growth remain robust or turn into either a soft or a hard landing? \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Asian giant's economy will be key to commodity prices - and therefore commodity-linked \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . It will also be important for Japanese exporters. Japan's trade with China is expected to have outpaced its trade with the U.S. for the first time in 2004. After U.S. growth, Chinese expansion was a main motor of 2004's 5.0% global growth - the best figure since 1973, according to the International Monetary Fund.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par   Will there be a coordinated effort to stem the dollar's slide? \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Some influential voices are calling for the U.S. to lead a globally coordinated effort to stem or slow dollar declines in the style of the Louvre Accord of 1985. That looks pretty unlikely right now with U.S. and Europe shouting at each other and China showing no hurry to move on the yuan. However, a disorderly dollar decline or a fall in U.S. bond or equity markets because of dollar weakness could change expectations.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par    Will there be a change in policy or personnel at the U.S. Treasury? \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Talk that Treasury Secretary John Snow would be asked to leave reached fever pitch after the election, though the White House later said he was staying. His support of a "strong dollar" with the caveat that markets should set its value has been broadly seen as a policy of benign neglect. Many pundits think that even if Snow does go, the Bush administration will maintain a hands off approach to the dollar unless it turns into a rout.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Laurence Norman and Steven Vames cover \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for Dow Jones Newswires in New York. Norman has previously covered the Argentine economy for Dow Jones in Buenos Aires. Vames has also covered equities in Los Angeles and the Treasurys market and fixed income industry in New York.)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - Laurence Norman, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-2096; laurence.norman@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Steven