\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  says did not  \highlight22\uc2 intervene \highlight\uc2  in  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  market in Dec.  \b0\par\par\uc2 426 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Dezember 2004\par 11:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Dec 30 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) said on Thursday it did not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in December despite the yen's rise to a five-year high against the U.S. dollar early in the month.  \par \par \uc2 MOF data showed the ministry stayed away from the markets from Nov. 27 to Dec. 28 - a stance that sharply contrasted with their aggressive dollar buying campaigns from January to mid-March this year.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar fell to a five-year low of 101.83 yen on Dec. 2, threatening to break below the psychologically important 100 yen mark, pressured by worries about the ability of the United States to finance its swelling current account deficit.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has bounced back since then and fetched around 103.80 yen in Tokyo trade on Thursday, mainly helped by position adjustments ahead of the New Year holidays.  \par \par \uc2 But dollar weakness has persisted, with the euro reaching a record high of $1.3647 on Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 Many traders expect the dollar to continue its descent next year, possibly making it tough for Japanese authorities, who fear too strong a yen will hurt Japanese exporters' overseas profits and derail the economy.  \par \par \uc2 The last time Japan stepped into the market was on March 16, when the dollar fell close to two yen on the day to around 108.60 yen.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese authorities spent a record 20 trillion yen ($192.7 billion) on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2003 and another 15 trillion yen in the first three months of 2004 - a huge amount that could offset the U.S. current account deficit of $164 billion in the July-September quarter.  \par \par \uc2 Compared to that period, the dollar is trading at much lower levels but traders say authorities in Tokyo seem less alarmed by the dollar's decline.  \par \par \uc2 Hiroshi Watanabe, vice finance minister for international affairs, said earlier this month that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  conducted by a single country was less effective than joint action.  \par \par \uc2 Finance Ministry officials have repeatedly said in recent weeks that they were ready to act if necessary. But many traders think \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unlikely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  soon - even if the dollar falls below the 100 yen mark.  \par \par \uc2 Although the yen has strengthened against the dollar - almost 8 percent in the last five months - the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has weakened against many other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the euro, mitigating the pain felt by Japanese exporters.  \par \par \uc2 The euro climbed to life-time high of 141.59 yen on Thursday. ($1=103.77 Yen).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041230e0cu00af7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
