\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar limps higher from all-time low vs euro.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Eric Burroughs  \par \uc2 678 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Dezember 2004\par 04:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Dec 28 (Reuters) - The dollar crawled up from a record low against the euro on Tuesday, though the market stayed focused on the U.S. deficits and a growing belief that central banks will not soon stand in the way of the dollar's decline.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese data showing industrial output rebounding less than expected in November and deflation deepening during the same month briefly pushed the yen lower against the dollar and euro.  \par \par \uc2 But other figures showed retail sales beating forecasts in November while the unemployment rate fell to 4.5 percent - the lowest in six years.  \par \par \uc2 Economists said the data showed Japan's economy struggling in the final months of the year but it also provided relief after months of consistently bad news and provided hope the slowdown in growth will be temporary.  \par \par \uc2 Still, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market remains fixated on the massive U.S. current account and budget deficits and the widespread belief a dollar decline is needed - and perhaps wanted by U.S. officials - to correct the imbalance.  \par \par \uc2 "Since October the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is not reacting much to these kinds of fundamental figures at all. It is focusing on the current account deficit of the U.S.," said Kikuko Takeda, manager of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi.  \par \par \uc2 David Gilmore, a partner at U.S.-based consultancy \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Analytics, said in a note to clients that the dollar's latest drop "is surely symptomatic of a growing consensus that the dollar downtrend has months more to play out".  \par \par \uc2 In midday Asia trade, the euro bought around $1.3600, down from late New York levels near $1.3620 and from its latest peak of $1.3640.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts had thought the $1.35 level would be a line in the sand for the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's tolerance of euro strength, but recent comments from officials have suggested the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not too worried.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar edged up to 103.20 yen, from around 103 yen at the New York close.  \par \par \uc2 The euro was little changed near 140.35 yen after rising on Monday to 140.74 yen, the highest level since June 2003 and close to an all-time high.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  AND SPECULATORS  \par \par \uc2 A warning from Japanese Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki on Tuesday that authorities will monitor \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets over the New Year holidays also helped push the yen slightly lower.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts generally believe Japan has become more tolerant of a stronger yen and would prefer to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in coordination with other countries.  \par \par \uc2 So far in 2004 the dollar has shed 8 percent versus the euro and the British pound and nearly 4 percent against the yen.  \par \par \uc2 Since the dollar's slide began in early 2002, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has lost more than half its value against the euro.  \par \par \uc2 But on a trade-weighted basis the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is down only about 30 percent since February 2002, according to the Federal Reserve's broad index, reflecting the heavy dollar-supportive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Asian central banks.  \par \par \uc2 With Christmas out of the way, some speculators were renewing their bets for the dollar to fall against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  after cutting back those short positions for most of December.  \par \par \uc2 "Non-Japanese traders are coming back to the market to sell dollars," BTM's Takeda said.  \par \par \uc2 Tokyo-based dealers said that with year-end holidays in Japan beginning on Wednesday for many people, speculative bets against the dollar were fewer in Tokyo than in the other trading centres.  \par \par \uc2 The latest data showed speculators in the Chicago futures market only trimmed their net short position in the euro for the week ending Dec. 21 - suggesting they have plenty of room to start dumping the dollar again, analysts said.  \par \par \uc2 Speculators still had sizeable net long positions in the yen and pound, eyeing further gains in those \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 London markets will remain closed on Tuesday but reopen on Wednesday, while New York markets will be open normal hours for almost the entire week.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041228e0cs003s5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
