\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ(12/27) Japan Has A  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Puzzle  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,109 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Dezember 2004\par 09:15\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2004, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 WSJ(12/27) Japan Has A \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Puzzle  \par \par \uc2 (From THE WALL STREET JOURNAL)  \par \par \uc2 By Phred Dvorak and Sebastian Moffett  \par \par \uc2 Tokyo -- FOR DECADES, WHENEVER the yen strengthened against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Japan's manufacturers would howl in protest. Now, Japanese companies produce so much overseas -- particularly in low-cost China -- they are a lot more ambivalent about where the yen should go.  \par \par \uc2 Far from bemoaning a strong yen -- which makes Japanese products more expensive on world markets -- some companies now are more worried about a weakening of the yen against the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the yuan.  \par \par \uc2 That new dynamic shows how complex \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  issues have become for manufacturers that export from production facilities around the globe. It also could prompt Tokyo to worry less about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to hold down the yen.  \par \par \uc2 For multinational manufacturers from around the world, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  concerns have focused on China in recent months as its trading partners raise pressure on Beijing to loosen yuan's peg to the dollar and let it trade more freely. Many expect the yuan to strengthen with any loosening -- something that would make Chinese products more expensive and help other countries to compete. But big companies that have invested in Chinese production also would see their Chinese costs rise.  \par \par \uc2 For Japanese companies, Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  considerations are particularly complex, since many export as much to China as they buy from it. Many are watching their own internal trade balances closely. Switch- and relay-maker Omron Corp., which is expanding production in China, is making sure it sells and buys roughly the same amount from sources in China, thus insulating itself better from \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swings.  \par \par \uc2 Japan's manufacturers have been moving production overseas for nearly two decades, since the yen started rising appreciably against other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Matsushita Electric Industrial Co., for instance, has factories in around 20 countries besides Japan; Sony Corp. has them in 14.  \par \par \uc2 China, which is turning into the world's factory floor as well as one of the fastest-growing markets for goods, has become a favorite factory destination. Matsushita has 46 factories in China that produce everything from plasma televisions to air conditioners. About half those goods are sold inside China and half exported. This year, for the first time, China will overtake the U.S. as Japan's largest trading partner.  \par \par \uc2 Of course, manufacturers from other countries increasingly are locating plants in China, too. The European Union and the U.S. each invested about $4 billion in China in 2003. European exports to China have increased strongly this year, particularly sales of capital goods such as specialized machine tools made by midsize German companies. China since last year has been South Korea's largest export market.  \par \par \uc2 But for now anyway, U.S. companies don't face fluctuations in their home \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the dollar-pegged yuan. And Europe's corporations have lagged behind those from Japan in investing in China, with many industries instead using low-wage countries in Eastern Europe as their low-cost base for re-exporting goods to Western Europe. South Korean conglomerates, including Samsung, LG and Hyundai, have made large investments in China, but their China \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  are geared to sales within China. That means, in general, that European and Korean manufacturers largely would benefit from a stronger yuan versus their own \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Japan's increasingly complicated manufacturing and sales relationship with China is making managers' \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  considerations more complex.  \par \par \uc2 Sony is typical. The electronics titan makes products in its own factories in China, largely for the Chinese market; it hires Chinese subcontractors to make Sony-brand gadgets such as CD Walkmans for export; and it sells Japan-made parts such as chips for digital cameras to its factories in China for both domestic sale and export.  \par \par \uc2 Some of those activities would benefit from a stronger yuan versus the yen; others would suffer.  \par \par \uc2 The ambivalence shows in a survey carried out a year ago by the Japan External Trade Organization, a government-backed agency that promotes trade. Asked how a hypothetical 30% rise in the yuan versus the yen would affect their businesses, about 26% of the 876 Japanese corporate respondents said it would be good because it would reduce the competitiveness of Chinese goods in overseas markets -- the traditional response. About 32% said it would be good because it would increase their price competitiveness in China, and so enable them to export more to China.  \par \par \uc2 Yet many of these same respondents -- and about 43% in all -- cited both pluses and minuses from a rise in the yuan, while 10% saw only minuses. The biggest reasons: 29.5% of all respondents said their profits would be hurt by the rise in price of goods they import from China, and 28.9% said they would suffer from the rise in price of materials and components they procure in China.  \par \par \uc2 Copier maker Fuji Xerox Co., which is an affiliate of Xerox Corp. and which on Nov. 1 announced a one billion yen, or about $96.5 million, expansion of its Shanghai production facilities, is one company concerned about a strengthening yuan. The increase will boost production capacity there to 300,000 by the fiscal year starting April 1, from 90,000 copiers a year. By then, it plans to have moved 90% of its production to China.  \par \par \uc2 Asked about the risks of locating so much production in China, Fuji Xerox President Toshio Arima recently said, 'The biggest risk is the problem of a rise in the yuan.'  \par \par \uc2 For the Japanese government, such concerns may lead to a less-\b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy. In the 15 months through March, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold a record 35 trillion yen -- almost all for dollars -- in an effort to stem the yen's rise. Since then, financial authorities have stood by as the dollar weakened to a five-year low of less than 102 yen on Dec. 2. Late Thursday in New York, the dollar was at 103.58 yen.  \par \par \uc2 Officials at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which directs exchange-rate policy, have said they are holding off on \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  because they would rather wait to coordinate with other countries. Economists say the ministry simply isn't so concerned right now about the effects of the strong yen. 'One reason there's been no \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is that, because of overseas production, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has only a small effect on consolidated earnings,' says Nikko Citigroup economist Kiichi Murashima.  \par \par \uc2 ---  \par \par \uc2 Marcus Walker in Frankfurt and Don Greenlees in Seoul contributed to this article.  \par \par \uc2 ---  \par \par \uc2 Journal Link: WSJ.com subscribers can see a rundown of who wins and loses with a weak dollar, at WSJ.com/JournalLinks.  \par \par \uc2 -0-   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020041227e0cr0058y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
