\par\par \b \uc2 Euro Breaks Resistance At $1.36 To Hit New Record High  \b0\par\par\uc2 522 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Dezember 2004\par 16:54\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--The dollar continued its slide in thin, technically- driven holiday trading Monday, reaching a fresh all-time low versus the euro as well as its lowest level versus the yen in almost three weeks.  \par \par \uc2 The euro snapped above $1.3600, with $1.3637 its latest in a series of historical highs for the day. By breaching $1.36, the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  took out what traders said was a level that had offered the dollar some options-related support.  \par \par \uc2 At 11.30 a.m. EST (1630 GMT), the euro was fetching $1.3635 from $1.3530 late Friday. The dollar was around a session low of Y103.10 from Y103.62, the lowest point for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  since December 8.  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the dollar was at CHF1.1334 from CHF1.1440. The pound was at $1.9362 from $1.9238, with both sterling and the Swiss franc at their best levels for the day.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said the dollar losses may continue, but that it was already an exaggerated movement in very thin end-of-year trading.  \par \par \uc2 Markets are closed Monday in the U.K., Canada, Australia and New Zealand among others. [ 12-27-04 1132ET ]  \par \par \uc2 There were different explanations for the dollar's fall. One trader at a leading \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  bank said that some speculative investors, who usually stay out the market until the new year, had come back in, sniffing an opportunity for profits they could not miss.  \par \par \uc2 "It's a little bit too painful for them not to be in at full steam," that trader said.  \par \par \uc2 Thomas Molloy, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at Bank Leumi in New York, said some of the dollar selling may stem from speculative investors becoming short euros earlier this month after taking profits on weeks of dollar losses.  \par \par \uc2 But as the dollar continued to decline, those investors have now been forced to cover positions by selling dollars and buying back euros. That was enough to counter efforts by the few traders in the market to defend options at $1.36.  \par \par \uc2 Molloy said that with $1.36 taken out, there was not an obvious resistance point before $1.37, if traders continue selling. However, he said the dollar's declines were already "exaggerated".  \par \par \uc2 "What's happening here is based on very little liquidity. If one guy cries wolf, everybody follows," he said.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar's declines could lead traders to ponder the possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The Y103-mark is seen as a possible trigger for action from the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while some said the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may consider \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  around $1.35.  \par \par \uc2 However, analysts have given a number of economic and political reasons why Japanese authorities may prefer to stay out of the market. That was reflected in early December when the dollar fell below Y102 without the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  ordering the BoJ to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Analysts also say the Europeans are very unlikely to step in alone.  \par \par \uc2 -By Laurence Norman, Dow Jones Newswires; laurence.norman@dowjones.com, 201-938-2096 [ 12-27-04 1153ET ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020041227e0cr0000h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
