\par\par \b \uc2 =DJ  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  VIEW: Dollar's Outlook Not As Gloomy In 2005  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Robert Flint  \par \uc2 954 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Dezember 2004\par 03:04\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2004, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--The dollar looks likely to continue its long-term decline in 2005, but the new year also holds the prospect of a gentler ride for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , particularly in the second half.  \par \par \uc2 After three years of slipping and sliding, the dollar may regain some of its footing if, as expected, the U.S. economy outperforms most of its major rivals.  \par \par \uc2 But dollar bulls, who were pushed to the verge of extinction in 2004, still remain an endangered species. Analysts generally expect the euro to trade between $1.35 and $1.40 in 12 months time. Late Tuesday, the euro stood at $1.3375. This compares with an all-time high of $1.3470 on Dec. 7 and $1.2579 at the end of 2003.  \par \par \uc2 'Our long-term forecasts remain (dollar) bearish and we keep our long-standing $1.40 twelve-month call on (euro/dollar),' wrote UBS in a research note.  \par \par \uc2 UBS, one of the largest participants in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, said it expects the dollar will need to fall further in the long term to help adjust the U.S. current account deficit and central banks will continue to diversify their reserves out of the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Concerns about financing the shortfalls resulting from structural imbalances in the U.S. economy have provided the rationale for much of the dollar's drop in recent years. Some indications that central banks are beginning to reduce the proportion of their \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves held in dollars also added pressure.  \par \par \uc2 While these worries aren't about to disappear off the investment horizon, they may lose a lot of their impact. Traders may take the dollar lower during the early months of 2005, but it's no longer a certain bet the greenback will continue to fall.  \par \par \uc2 'While the prospects for new lows for the (dollar) in early 2005 remain strong, there are glimmers of hope...for renewed (dollar) gains, particularly versus European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , during the final three quarters of next year,' wrote Robert Sinche, head of global \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Bank of America in New York.  \par \par \uc2 The economic picture for the U.S. next year is widely considered to be more promising than for the euro zone or Japan. The Federal Reserve 'will be hiking interest rates more aggressively than other central banks in 2005,' according to Carl Weinberg, chief economist at High Frequency Economics, an advisory group based in Valhalla, N.Y.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. dollar interest rates will 'continue to pull above those of much of the rest of the world,' Weinberg wrote in an outlook for 2005. 'This will make it increasingly expensive to short the U.S. dollar, a distinct change from 2004, when speculators were paid to bet the U.S. dollar would cheapen.'  \par \par \uc2 After the increase announced on Dec. 12, the Fed's benchmark rate stands at 2.25% against an equivalent euro-zone rate of 2.0% and near zero in Japan. Many analysts expect the key U.S. rate to reach between 3.0% and 4.0% by the end of 2005.  \par \par \uc2 A return 'to chase U.S. yield,' coupled with the difficulty that major \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  pairs face in breaking through historical highs or lows, 'will restrain the underlying dollar bear next year,' said Amarjit Sahota, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at the San Francisco office of London-based HIFX, a corporate advisory firm.  \par \par \uc2 In fact, the dollar's losses during 2004 were modest compared with 2003. The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is down about 6.3% this year versus the euro after a 20% drop in 2003.  \par \par \uc2 Against the yen, the dollar faces a somewhat similar situation next year. Analysts are looking for a modest decline for the dollar over the next 12 months to about Y102.50. Late Tuesday, the dollar was changing hands at Y104.33.  \par \par \uc2 Although Japanese monetary authorities haven't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in markets to slow the yen's appreciation since March this year, few observers doubt the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will hesitate to use the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  weapon again if the dollar dips below Y100 at any point.  \par \par \uc2 The China Wild Card  \par \par \uc2 One factor complicating the outlook for the interplay between the dollar, yen and euro is the fate of China's de facto peg of the yuan to the dollar. Should the Chinese leadership decide during 2005 to loosen or replace the band that keeps the dollar at CNY8.2770, the yuan could appreciate anywhere from 3% to 10%, according to analysts. This, in turn, would create room for a corresponding dollar depreciation against other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 However, it's by no means certain China will abandon a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime that has served it well for the past 10 years. The timing and extent of any alteration are matters of pure speculation.  \par \par \uc2 As always, unknowable and therefore unpredictable developments can influence the dollar next year.  \par \par \uc2 'In addition to the usual economic risks, there are perhaps a bigger number of geopolitical risks out there also for investors to think about for the year ahead and beyond,' wrote the research team at Goldman Sachs in a recent email update.  \par \par \uc2 Iraq and tensions in the Middle East, as well as Afghanistan, the Korean peninsula and the Straits of Taiwan all serve as flash points, while terrorist activities across the globe continue to pose threats.  \par \par \uc2 (Robert Flint, an assistant news editor, has covered \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets for Dow Jones Newswires for the past seven years and was previously Beijing bureau chief and chief correspondent in Scandinavia.)  \par \par \uc2 -Robert Flint; Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-4408; robert.flint@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -0-   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020041222e0cm001mf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
