\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  VIEW: Mkt Forces Still Share Power In  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  World  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,073 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Dezember 2004\par 21:28\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Laurence Norman \par    A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Over the past 12 months, the Bush administration has consistently allowed its call for markets to set \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates to trump its avowal of a strong dollar policy.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 The result has been a third year of dollar declines, taking the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to levels where investors are left scratching their heads, or rolling their eyes, when Treasury Secretary John Snow repeats the strong dollar script.  \par \par \uc2 Yet while the strong dollar policy has long been written off by investors, there has been less focus on the growing obstacles to the administration's hopes for unfettered \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.  \par \par \uc2 From Buenos Aires to Beijing and Kuala Lumpur to Kuwait City, the hand of government is becoming more, not less, visible in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. That's despite a string of communiques from the G7 group of industrialized nations signing onto the free market ideal. It also flies in the face of influential voices at the top level of international financial circles who call for flexible exchange rates to help countries absorb macroeconomic shocks and remove distortions from global trade.  \par \par \uc2 The most talked about practitioners of fettering the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets were once again the Asian countries in 2004. China's de facto peg against the dollar made it into the U.S. presidential race, with Democratic candidate John Kerry accusing President George W. Bush of refusing to "stand up and take on China for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation."  \par \par \uc2 With China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  staying at CNY8.2770 to the dollar, other Asian countries continued to fear that further appreciation of their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  versus the dollar would squeeze their exporters. Foreign investors would then likely head to China's special economic zones and ignore Seoul, Bangkok and Jakarta.  \par \par \uc2 In the first quarter of 2004, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  massively, spent almost 15 trillion yen to buy dollars. The Bank of Japan has stayed out the market since, but as the yen reached four-year highs last month, officials have made it clear they stand ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Korea has shown no such hesitation. In a bid to stem the won ($1=KRW1053.5), the country had issued KRW18 trillion in government bonds as of Dec. 3 to fund dollar purchases in the market.  \par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, the governor of Thailand's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Pridiyathorn Devakula, was quoted by international news agencies saying the bank will "try to maintain the baht's exchange rate to help make exports competitive."  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Hong Kong has kept its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board and peg to the U.S. dollar. The Malaysian ringgit is still fixed to the dollar, with senior officials this week dousing down expectations of a policy change.  \par \par \uc2 Asia Does Not Stand Alone  \par \par \uc2 While Asia gets most of the attention, it is not the only region seeking to stem dollar depreciation.  \par \par \uc2 In all the talk about China's dollar peg, it is often forgotten that most oil-producing Middle East countries have fixed exchange rates, including Saudi Arabia and Kuwait.  \par \par \uc2 Ali Rada, president of Radaforex, an independent interdealer broker in New York, said that with oil prices at current levels, the Middle Eastern petroleum-exporting countries are "bloated with dollar income" and their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be "a lot higher than they are now." He said "politics" likely explains why there is no pressure from Europe, Japan or the U.S. for a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  revaluation.  \par \par \uc2 Latin American countries are the most recent members to join - in fact, to rejoin - the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  club. Argentina has been buying dollars for almost two years. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  says the purchases are aimed at meeting money supply targets, while everyone else thinks such measures are aimed at helping exporters.  \par \par \uc2 Last month, Brazil publicly announced it would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it would do so to build reserves, but the move came as the real reached a 30-month high and most analysts believe the policy is a helping hand for the country's powerful industrial lobby. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will not say how much it's bought, but reserves have risen $1 billion in the last month, having stayed almost flat from Aug 21-Nov. 21.  \par \par \uc2 The latest convert is Colombia, which has spent over $3 billion to stem the peso's 17% rise this year. Last week, it imposed so-called "hot money" capital controls, cut interest-rates and threatened further measures if the peso continues to climb.  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, as the euro has reached all-time highs, the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has warned it may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the euro zone economy starts to bear an unfair burden of dollar weakness. That would be the first time in at least two and a half years that the ECB has stepped in.  \par \par \uc2 As for 2005, Stephen Jen, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Morgan Stanley in London, said in a recent research report there's little sign that authorities will become more laissez-faire.  \par \par \uc2 Jen wrote that in an ideal world, there would be a global coordinated approach to deal with the U.S. current account deficit - the chief culprit behind dollar weakness. That could take some pressure off the dollar and lift the pressure off central banks to step in. Yet Jen said that is unlikely, because with the U.S. aiming for the highest possible "headline growth", Asia "insistent on maximizing its current account surpluses to prevent ... excess savings from degenerating into strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 " and Europe unwilling to push through radical pro-market reforms, "a war of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely in 2005."  \par \par \uc2 Jen said this "war" could involve "level-defending" \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the ECB and Japanese authorities, not mere volatility smoothing.  \par \par \uc2 So is John Snow alone in fighting for free \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets? Not so. The U.K., Sweden, Canada, Australia and Mexico, among others, have also followed a hands-off approach.  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile Snow seems to have a soul-mate in Ingimundur Fridriksson, assistant governor of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Iceland. Fridriksson told Dow Jones Newswires in a telephone interview Wednesday that since the krona was floated in 2001, the bank has "not tried to influence the rate."  \par \par \uc2 "We live by what the market dictates," he added.  \par \par \uc2 (Laurence Norman covers \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  for Dow Jones Newswires in New York. He has previously covered the Argentine economy for Dow Jones in Buenos Aires.)  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -Laurence Norman, Dow Jones Newswires, 1-201-938-2096; laurence.norman@dowjones.com .-0- \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 12-22-04 1528ET ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020041222e0cm0003e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
