\par\par \b \uc2 SNAPSHOT-Bank views on Asia's 2005 economic prospects.  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,222 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Dezember 2004\par 11:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE/HONG KONG, Dec 21 (Reuters) - Following are extracts from recent investment bank reports and presentations on the outlook for Asia's economies, \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and markets in 2005.  \par \par \uc2 CREDIT SUISSE FIRST BOSTON (Dec 21)  \par \par \uc2 - Asia ex-Japan to grow 6.4 percent in 2005 and 6.7 percent in 2006 on the back of 7.8 percent growth this year.  \par \par \uc2 - Beijing likely to opt for widening of the yuan's band instead of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket peg. Move may happen once "hot money" inflows slow, probably in the first half of 2005, and will be followed by a 3-5 percent appreciation in the yuan's value.  \par \par \uc2 - China's GDP growth to moderate to 7.3 percent in 2005 from 9.5 percent this year before picking up to 7.5 percent in 2006.  \par \par \uc2 "We look for hard landing in investment, soft landing in GDP growth and no landing in consumption," said Dong Tao, chief regional economist at CSFB.  \par \par \uc2 - Malaysia will not change the ringgit peg in 2005.  \par \par \uc2 - Hong Kong dollar peg to remain unchanged even if yuan de-pegged, as fundamentals do not warrant a change.  \par \par \uc2 CALYON (Dec 17)  \par \par \uc2 - Asia excluding Japan to expand by 6.7 percent in 2005 after 7.5 percent this year.  \par \par \uc2 - China growth to slow to 8.0 percent from 9.2 percent.  \par \par \uc2 - End of textile quotas in 2005 will be a key event for Asia. China is likely to be the big winner, tripling its share of global textile and garment trade to over 50 percent in the years to come. India should also benefit to a lesser extent.  \par \par \uc2 MERRILL LYNCH (Dec 14)  \par \par \uc2 - Growth in Asia to be hampered by slower growth in the United States and China, while domestic demand will be resilient.  \par \par \uc2 - Inflationary pressures mounting after more than two years of above-trend growth and rising capacity utilisation.  \par \par \uc2 - Deflation in Japan and is expected to disappear in 2005.  \par \par \uc2 - China to grow 8.0 percent in 2005 vs 9.2 percent in 2004.  \par \par \uc2 LEHMAN BROTHERS (Dec 13)  \par \par \uc2 - Asia ex-Japan GDP to grow 6.5 percent in 2005 and 6.7 percent in 2006 after 7.2 percent in 2004.  \par \par \uc2 - Bullish vs GDP consensus on Hong Kong, bearish on Philippines.  \par \par \uc2 - Asia's combined current account surplus to narrow to 2.7 percent of GDP in 2005 and 0.9 percent in 2006 from 4.1 percent in 2004.  \par \par \uc2 - The yuan will be allowed to appreciate 5 percent by the end of 2005; other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  will rise by 10-20 percent; Malaysia will abandon the ringgit peg in second half of 2005.  \par \par \uc2 "Our outlook is that 2005 will be the biggest inflection point for the region since the Asian crisis - in various ways, it will be like the Asian crisis in reverse."  \par \par \uc2 "Overall, we judge that Asia ex-Japan is about to enter a new paradigm. Spurred by growing self confidence, the region will emerge decisively from the shadow of the Asian crisis. The new paradigm will entail significant Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation, robust domestic demand, and narrowing current account surpluses."  \par \par \uc2 STANDARD CHARTERED (Dec 10)  \par \par \uc2 - The yuan will start a "steady snake-like" appreciation. China will raise interest rates further in 2005. Its GDP will grow 8.0 percent after 9.3 percent in 2004.  \par \par \uc2 - Malaysia will change its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg. Hong Kong will not.  \par \par \uc2 "Instead of focusing on external competitiveness, the broad-based U.S. dollar decline will allow central banks to focus on domestic demand by either cutting rates, or at least keeping interest rates low.  \par \par \uc2 "This would not only help insulate the Asian economy from a likely external slowdown, but it would also help to reduce global current account imbalances and to boost global growth over the long term."  \par \par \uc2 The biggest economic risk is the danger of a synchronised downturn in the U.S. and China. Another known shock is the ending of global textile quotas, which will hit low value-added producers like Bangladesh and help China, India and Pakistan.  \par \par \uc2 JP MORGAN (Dec 10)  \par \par \uc2 - Dollar will decline and U.S. rates will rise steadily.  \par \par \uc2 - Asian bonds will outperform U.S. Treasuries. Asian dollar bonds are appealing as economic fundamentals improve and sovereign ratings get upgraded.  \par \par \uc2 - Equities in Asia to continue to do better than bonds.  \par \par \uc2 - Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation to persist; top picks are the Taiwan dollar and Thai baht.  \par \par \uc2 GOLDMAN SACHS (Dec 8)  \par \par \uc2 - Asia ex-Japan GDP to grow 6.8 percent in 2005 after 8.2 percent in 2004.  \par \par \uc2 - China to slow to 8.1 percent from 9.7 percent.  \par \par \uc2 "Our view is that the renminbi will go through a regime change sometime in the first half of next year, potentially as early as the first quarter," said Sun Bae Kim, head of Asia economic research.  \par \par \uc2 "The renminbi will go through a one-off strengthening against the U.S. dollar, whether it is closer to 2.5 percent or 5 percent, and then subsequently be managed against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the near term."  \par \par \uc2 - Goldman remains "overweight" China stocks  \par \par \uc2 ING (Dec 8)  \par \par \uc2 The threat from Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  asset diversification is overstated because, except for Japan, banks have already been buying fewer Treasuries all year. Yet yields have hardly budged.  \par \par \uc2 "We do not see Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  behaviour threatening higher U.S. Treasury yields, which leaves as the main threat the outlook for U.S. growth and inflation," said Asian chief economist Tim Condon.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again on a massive scale if the yen rises. The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be successful and, as before, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will invest the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  proceeds in U.S. Treasuries.  \par \par \uc2 Asia will enjoy robust, domestic demand-driven growth in 2005 that will narrow output gaps and boost investment demand.  \par \par \uc2 Asian dollar bond issuance as a result will approach or even exceed 2004's total of around $30 billion. But excess liquidity and credit rating upgrades should compress spreads further.  \par \par \uc2 MORGAN STANLEY (Dec 6)  \par \par \uc2 - Asia ex-Japan GDP to grow 5.9 percent in 2005 and 5.6 percent in 2006 versus 7.1 percent in 2004.  \par \par \uc2 - China GDP to slow to 7.8 percent in 2005 and 7.0 percent in 2006 after 9.3 percent in 2004.  \par \par \uc2 "It is already apparent that the preference for a weak dollar from the U.S. and China's reluctance to raise interest rates will likely prolong the global liquidity and growth boom into 1H05.  \par \par \uc2 "This would make 2005 similar to 2004, where consumer and investment demand benefited from the liquidity boom that started in 3Q03."  \par \par \uc2 "We see the correction in China's boom-bust cycle continuing into 2006 despite a stronger global economy, clouding growth prospects also for Hong Kong and Taiwan."  \par \par \uc2 CITIGROUP (Dec 3)  \par \par \uc2 - Asia-Pacific GDP to grow 6.3 percent in 2005 and 6.4 percent in 2006 versus 7.2 percent this year.  \par \par \uc2 - \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to rise as growth stays solid, uncertainty about U.S. rates is reduced. Sharper China slowdown is a risk.  \par \par \uc2 - Regional yields and rates to rise but the process will be measured and manageable.  \par \par \uc2 - Equities to do well; highest earnings and dividend growth rates will be in the domestic, not the export, sector.  \par \par \uc2 "The basic Asian economic forecast is one of GDP growth and trade balances consolidating against a backdrop of modest inflation pressure, creeping interest rates and stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041221e0cl00avr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
