\par\par \b \uc2 Dow Jones Asia-Pacific Top Stories Sked For Dec. 10  \b0\par\par\uc2 2,266 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Dezember 2004\par 10:22\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 Following are the top Asia-Pacific stories featured on Dow Jones Newswires as of 0900 GMT, along with items expected later in the day. Please direct any questions or comments to asianewseds@dowjones.com or by phone on 65-6415-4020.  \par \par \uc2 MACRO/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /FIXED INCOME  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: China's consumer price inflation slowed to 2.8% year on year in November, the lowest rate in 12 months, calling into question whether the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will keep raising interest rates. ("=DATA VIEW: China Nov CPI At 12-Mo Low; May Affect Rates," ran at 0703 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: Australia's trade deficit widened in October, suggesting the chronic trade imbalance will continue to depress economic growth in the fourth quarter. ("=UPDATE:Australia Trade Deficit Widens; Likely Drag On 4Q," ran at 0442 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: China's State Administration of \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Friday said that short-term \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fluctuations wouldn't prompt a change in the composition of the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves. (Story to run shortly)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japan's domestic corporate goods prices rose on year for the ninth straight month in November, but there aren't yet any signs that the higher upstream costs will flow to consumer prices. ("=UPDATE: Gains In Japan CGPI Still Far From Lifting CPI," ran at 0558 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 Australia Corp Bonds: Australia's corporate bond market approached the end of a red hot 2004 in style this week with a successful deal for National Australia Bank Ltd. (NAB). ("=Australia Corp Bonds: NAB Closes Year With Red Hot Deal," ran at 0506 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: An oddity of South Korea's volatile interest rate derivatives market has begun to correct - good news for bold investors who had bet economic fundamentals would eventually prevail. Korea's "\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basis," the gap between interest-rate and cross-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps, is widening after it shriveled several weeks ago to its narrowest ever. ("ASIA DEBT: Bold Korea \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Basis Bets Paying Off" to run shortly)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: China's recent interest rate increase hasn't cooled residents' desire to buy houses, a quarterly survey by the People's Bank of China shows. (Story to run by around 1045 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: South Korea's power giant, Korea Electric Power Corp., sold Y20 billion of three-year Samurai bonds Friday in what was likely the last offering in the market for 2004. ("=UPDATE: Kepco Offers Y20B 3-Year In Last Samurai Of 2004," ran at 0856 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 NEW DELHI: India's industrial output grew at a seven-year high rate of 10.1% in October, fed by strong investment and consumer demand, while inflation remained above 7% for the fifth consecutive month, according to government data released Friday. The numbers make a case for a modest rise in overnight interest rates, say analysts. (UPDATE to run by 1030 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: South Korea's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Economy said Friday it will sell on Monday 800 billion won in five-year treasurys aimed at stabilizing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. With the new issue, the government will reach its entire KRW18.8 trillion limit for treasury issues this year to raise funds via the local market to stabilize the Korean won, the ministry said in a statement. ("S Korea Govt To Hit Full-Yr KRW18.8T Ceiling For \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Tsys," first take ran at 0521 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  should \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  "decisively" in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets should the yen appreciate further as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise could push the country's flagging economy into recession, according to a senior economist at a major life insurer. ("=INTERVIEW: Must Stop Yen Rise To Avert Slump - Economist," ran at 0259 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Asia: With the margin of New Zealand bond yields over their equivalent Australian credits unlikely to widen further as the Australian economy recovers, RBC Capital Markets believes the Australian dollar can rise from a near nine-year low against its commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  cousin. ("=\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Asia:Buy AUD/NZD On Better Australian Econ Data - RBC," ran at 0249 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =China Money Mkts Week: The People's Bank of China sent a strong signal this week it intends to capture more banking system liquidity, more permanently. ("=China Money Mkts Week: PBOC Widens Capital Capture Net," ran at 0323 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: New Zealand's terms of trade index, a measure of international purchasing power, fell in the third quarter from the second after export prices declined more than import prices. ("DATA SNAP: NZ Terms Of Trade, Export Volumes Fall In 3Q" ran at 2148 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 EQUITIES  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Singapore's High Court Friday threw a six-week lifeline to troubled China Aviation Oil Singapore Corp., as the jet-fuel supplier prepares a rescue plan and seeks ways to repay creditors following Asia's worst financial scandal this year. ("Singapore Crt OKs 6-Weeks Extension For China Aviation," first take ran at 0825 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: The ultimate government shareholder of China Aviation Oil (Singapore) Corp. Friday gave its first public response to the company's massive derivative trading losses, but gave no sign that it would immediately \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the affair. ("China Govt Awaiting Resolution Of CAO Singapore Losses," ran at 0855 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 PERTH: The takeover fight for WMC Resources Ltd. turned nasty Friday as predator Xstrata PLC attacked the Australian miner's plan to return A$1 billion to shareholders. ("=UPDATE: Xstrata Attacks "Illusory" Benefits Of WMC Plan," ran at 0753 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: U.K. insurance and investment firm HHG PLC said Friday it plans to sell its life services business for GBP1.03 billion to concentrate on its funds management \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . ("=UPDATE: HHG Plans To Sell UK Life Svcs Ops For GBP1.03B," ran at 0714 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: New China Life Insurance Co. said Friday it plans to raise about CNY1.3 billion through a subordinated debt issue, which would complement an initial public offering that has been delayed because of a suspension in new listings in China. Meanwhile, China Pacific Insurance (Group) Co., one of the country's largest insurers, has decided to postpone its planned IPO citing China's current macroeconomic environment, a senior company official told Dow Jones Newswires on Friday. (UPDATE to run by around 1115 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Beijing Media Corp., which handles advertisements for newspapers in the Beijing Youth Daily group, set a price range of HK$14.95 to HK$18.95 a share for its initial public offering, a person familiar with the deal said Friday. ("Beijing Media IPO Range HK$14.95-HK$18.95/Shr - Source," ran at 0301 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI: Shares of Glaxo Consumer surged 7% Friday after the company said it would buy back 7.3% of its share capital, or 3.3 million shares, at INR370 each, a 10% premium. (UPDATE to run by around 1200 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: China's broadcast-media regulator will draft new rules intended to keep offensive advertising off the nation's television screens, the official Xinhua news agency reported. ("China To Draft New Rules To Stop Offensive TV Commercials," ran at 0146 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: Dutch specialty-chemicals company DSM NV has received permission from a China government agency to acquire a CNY206.6 million stake in North China Pharmaceutical Co. ("China Agency OKs Dutch DSM CNY206.6M China Pharma Deal," ran at 0101 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MELBOURNE: National Australia Bank Ltd. paid almost A$50 million to twelve current and former executives over the past year, including A$10.4 million to new Australian \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  boss Ahmed Fahour. ("=Australia's NAB Executives Swimming in A$50M Pay Pool," ran at 0209 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Construction-use crane maker Tadano Ltd. said Friday that it is recalling some 10,000 crane vehicles after a faulty steering system caused a fatal accident in August. ("Tadano Recalls 10,000 Crane Vehicles After Fatal Accident," ran at 0525 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Mazda Motor Corp. said Friday it has set up a new unit in Mexico to start selling vehicles there, taking advantage of the scheduled implementation of a free trade agreement between Japan and Mexico next year. ("Mazda To Start Selling Cars In Mexico; Planned FTA Helps," ran at 0329 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Seibu Railway Co., which is to be delisted from the Tokyo bourse next week, said Friday it will postpone its plan to list on a smaller market by the end of next March. ("Seibu Railway Gives Up Plan To List On Jasdaq By March," ran at 0833 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japan conquered the worst of its banking mess in 2004. Next year, it may boast the world's largest bank. ("=OUTLOOK 05: Japan Looks Set To Have World's Biggest Bank," ran at 0701 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =Asian Stock Focus: NEC Corp. surprise move to buy back two listed subsidiaries sparked a short-term jump for its share price - but also some concerns about the Japanese electronics giant's long-term strategy. ("=Asian Stock Focus: Japan NEC Subsidiary Buyback Queried," ran at 0302 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MELBOURNE: With large acquisitions off the agenda, Australian drinks company Foster's Group Ltd. may give back up to A$1.5 billion in spare money to shareholders. ("=UPDATE: Foster's May Return A$1.5B To Shareholders," ran at 0635 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: The enormous scale of their \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the old-fashioned mentality of their management are the two biggest challenges facing China's banks, says an executive from IBM Business Consulting Services. ("=INTERVIEW: IBM Consulting: China Bks Face Culture Clash," ran at 0433 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: South Korea's National Agricultural Cooperative Federation is assessing plans for a possible takeover of ailing LG Card Co., said an official at the financial institution Friday. ("S Korea NACF Studying Plans For Possible LG Card Takeover," ran at 0113 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: South Korea's Ssangyong Motor Co. said Friday it will sell at least 5,300 units of its New Rexton sport utility vehicle by 2010 to Iran's Morattab Industrial Manufacturing Co. ("S Korea's Ssangyong Motor To Sell New Rexton SUVs In Iran," ran at 0340 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: China's top securities regulator has pledged to support the development of the fund sector and encourage more institutional investors to enter the country's capital markets, major financial dailies report Friday. ("China Securities Chief Vows To Support Devt Of Funds," ran at 0249 GMT) [ 10-12-04 0922GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Shipments of beer Japanese brewers fell 4.8% on-year in volume terms in January-November period, reinforcing the possibility that data for the full year will drop below the previous year's level for the third straight year, beer industry officials said Friday. (Story to run shortly)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: Discount retailer The Warehouse Group is confident it won't alienate its traditional bargain-hunting customers as it puts more emphasis on quality at its core Red Sheds \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the company's chief executive said Friday. ("=INTERVIEW:NZ Warehouse Says Quality Won't Deter Shoppers" ran at 0211 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: In the five months since listing on the New Zealand stock exchange, CanWest MediaWorks Ltd. has seen its profits and stock price surge. Now the radio and television network operator is seeking to further tighten its grip on the country's media market. ("=UPDATE: NZ CanWest Extends Strong Post-IPO Profit Growth," ran at 0310 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: A bidding war broke out Friday for little noticed rural services company Williams & Kettle, as three larger agricultural concerns sought strategic stakes in the company. ("=UPDATE: NZ Bidding War Breaks Out For Williams & Kettle" ran at 0228 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 ENERGY/COMMODITIES  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: South Korea and China plan to cooperate more closely on joint purchases, transportation and stockpiling of crude oil amid volatile global oil prices, according to the Federation of Korean Industries. ("S Korea,China To Work Together On Crude Oil Purchases," ran at 0610 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: China's launch of fuel oil futures trading in August 2004 has been a relatively successful affair which, in time, should pave the way for a range of crude and other refined products contracts. ("=OUTLOOK 05: China Mulls New Oil Futures, Timing Unclear," ran at 0436 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: A Toyota Motor Corp. executive said Friday the introduction of an environment tax discussed at a government panel is unlikely to contribute to reducing the fossil fuel consumption. ("Toyota Exec Says New Tax Will Not Reduce Fossil Fuel Use," ran at 0744 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: An exploration unit of Royal Dutch/Shell Group plans to set up a joint venture with the Bureau of Geological Exploration and Mineral Development of Jilin Province to conduct a field study of oil shale resources in the northeastern province, Shell China said. ("Shell To Form JV For Oil Shale Development In China," ran at 0400 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: China will provide the agriculture sector with more support in 2005, Ma Kai, the head of the National Development and Reform Commission said, according to a report from the official Xinhua News Agency. ("China To Increase Ag Sector Aid,Grain Acreage '05 -Xinhua," ran at 0507 GMT; see also "China May Scrap Farm Tax In '05,Ahead Of Schedule -Report" at 0518 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: Considered one of the most potential markets in the world, China will continue to have a big appetite for base metals, but consumption growth will slow in 2005 amid government efforts to moderate economic growth, analysts say. ("=OUTLOOK 05: China Metals Demand Strong, Growth To Slow," ran at 0400 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: New Zealand's Commerce Commission said Friday it has granted electricity networks company Vector clearance to purchase up to 100% of gas distribution and metering concern NGC Holdings. ("NZ Watchdog Clears Vector To Buy 100% Of NGC" ran at 2034 GMT) [ 10-12-04 0922GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 62550  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020041210e0ca000nm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
