\par\par \b \uc2 DJ UPDATE: Schroeder For Veto Rts For New UN Council Members  \b0\par\par\uc2 611 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Dezember 2004\par 21:04\par \uc2 OsterDowJones Commodity Wire\par \uc2 OSTDJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2004, Comtex News Network.  All Rights Reserved.  Standard Attribution Statement: News Provided by COMTEX.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Dec 09, 2004 (ODC via COMTEX) --  \par \par \uc2 New permanent members of an expanded U.N. Security Council should have the same veto rights as those who currently hold seats, German Chancellor Gerhard Schroeder said Thursday. Speaking at an economic forum during a trip to Japan, Schroeder said "double standards shouldn't be applied" if the Security Council were enlarged. Germany and Japan are both lobbying, together with Brazil and India, to win permanent seats on the U.N. body. Though Tokyo has long insisted that nations joining the U.K., China, France, Russia, and the U.S. should be awarded a veto, this is the first time Schroeder has mentioned the issue. After his speech, Schroeder said new members should have equal status. "That means of course that they, like the (permanent five), should have the veto right," he said. Japanese Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi backed that position at a joint news conference later in the day. An international panel Nov. 30 proposed two options to expand the Security Council from the current 15 members to 24 members. However, neither option would add any new veto-wielding members. One proposal from the panel would add six new permanent members - two from Asia, two from Africa, one from the Americas and one from Europe - as well as three non-permanent members elected for two-year terms. The other proposal would create a new tier of eight semi-permanent members chosen for four-year terms - two each from Asia, Africa, Europe and the Americas. It would also add one non-permanent seat. Schroeder added he hoped that two African nations would join Brazil, Germany, India, and Japan in their campaign. "I hope that we can invite two countries from the African continent to this initiative," he said. On global oil prices, the chancellor said he hoped there would be better means to ascertain demand for oil from the economies of China and India to stabilize prices, adding he hoped the Group of Eight countries would cooperate to boost market transparency. "I think the time has come that the G8 states take a common initiative for more transparency on the oil markets. I will soon make such proposals to my colleagues," Schroeder said without elaborating. "We think that with more transparency in key price influences for example the demand situation in China and India, we must remove the basis for speculation." The chancellor briefly weighed in on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  during his address, though avoided specifics about the U.S. dollar, which has fallen sharply against the yen and euro over the past two years. "I know about the independence of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But one hint I want to give is one can sometimes learn something from Japan and his excellent \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies," Schroeder said. He didn't elaborate, though Tokyo is known for actively selling yen for dollars on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to keep its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from rapidly appreciating. A weak dollar hurts Japanese exporters by lowering the value of their overseas earnings when converted back into yen. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  hasn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market since March, however, when it wound up a multibillion dollar campaign to keep the yen from strengthening. In the January-March quarter, Japan spent Y14.83 trillion buying dollars on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. Koizumi said he doesn't favor the rapid weakening of the U.S. dollar and that, "the international community should cooperate to stabilize exchange rates." "As for the merits of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , various opinions will have to be discussed," he added.  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Sharon Vong  \par \par \uc2 Copyright 2004 Dow Jones. All rights reserved.  \par \par \uc2 Dokument OSTDJ00020041210e0c900187\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
