\par\par \b \uc2 =DJ INTERVIEW:German Econ Secy: US Doing Nothing To Stop Dlr  \b0\par\par\uc2 By James Simms  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 681 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Dezember 2004\par 12:35\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2004, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--A senior German economic official Thursday questioned whether any possible joint \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be effective in changing the direction of the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 He also said that the euro's current near all-time high against the dollar would 'normally' call for concerted action by the ECB, Japan and the U.S.  \par \par \uc2 'It isn't sufficient if either of us would try to solve the problem alone. I wonder even if it would be sufficient if Japan and the ECB would do something in common,' said Bernd Pfaffenbach, state minister of the Federal Ministry of Economics and Labour, when asked about speculation over action by the Bank of Japan and the ECB.  \par \par \uc2 'Normally, given the situation at the time being, this would call for concerted action, if you want to change something,' he said.  \par \par \uc2 'And concerted action would mean the United States included, and at the time being, I don't see the intention from that side to come up with coordination,' the minister told Dow Jones Newswires in an interview.  \par \par \uc2 'I don't want to say that it's necessary to do something in common. What I want to say is, if you want to bring the exchange rate into another direction, it would necessarily be in a concerted way,' he added.  \par \par \uc2 German Chancellor Gerhard Schroeder earlier Thursday in a speech to business executives in Tokyo reiterated his concern over the euro's strength and said lessons could be learned from the BOJ's 'superb' policies - without elaborating. He added, however, that he didn't want to impinge on the independence of any \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including the ECB.  \par \par \uc2 In the 15 months through March of this year, the BOJ under the direction of Japan's Finance Ministry dumped Y35 trillion - almost all for dollars - to weaken the yen against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Pfaffenbach, who is also the chancellor's sherpa on Group of Eight issues, said he was 'surprised' by the frankness' of Schroeder and that the chancellor meant that he was concerned over Europe and Germany bearing the brunt of the impact from the U.S. dollar policy.  \par \par \uc2 'What (Schroeder) means is that we have a difficult situation at the time being. The American policy is in a situation where they do nothing against the weak dollar - to help bring their twin deficit into balance,' he said. 'In the end, Europe has to pay the price for that, and if you speak about Europe, the stronger economy of Germany also who has to suffer from that.'  \par \par \uc2 Pfaffenbach is traveling with Schroeder, who is on a week-long trip to China and Japan to discuss business and foreign policy issues.  \par \par \uc2 The minister also affirmed the government's real gross domestic product forecasts both for this year and next. The government forecasts GDP growth of 1.8% in 2004 after the economy shrank slightly by 0.1% in 2003. For 2005, it sees economic growth of 1.7%. Its range is for 1.5%-2% growth in each of 2004 and 2005.  \par \par \uc2 The German government has repeatedly said that the recovery in Europe's largest economy is strengthening, pointing to a pickup in equipment goods investment. It also sees domestic demand on the upturn.  \par \par \uc2 The minister said that he didn't expect Schroeder to bring up the topic of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  with Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi during their hour-long summit, but that he would be prepared to discuss it if needed.  \par \par \uc2 'The chancellor didn't come to discuss exchange rate problems, but if the prime minister would come up from his side, of course, he would be prepared,' Pfaffenbach said.  \par \par \uc2 Schroeder flew in to Japan from China Wednesday and will meet Koizumi later Thursday to discuss United Nations reforms, reconstruction in Iraq and economic ties. He is scheduled to leave Tokyo Friday.  \par \par \uc2 -By James Simms, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; james.simms@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Hugh Lawson  \par \par \uc2 -0-   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020041209e0c9008et\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
