\par\par \b \uc2 Dow Jones Asia-Pacific Top Stories Sked For Dec 9  \b0\par\par\uc2 2,549 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Dezember 2004\par 10:37\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 Following are the top Asia-Pacific stories featured on Dow Jones Newswires as of 0900 GMT, along with items expected later in the day. Please direct any questions or comments to asianewseds@dowjones.com or by phone on 65-6415-4020.  \par \par \uc2 MACRO/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /FIXED INCOME  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: Acknowledging for the first time that the South Korean economy faces prolonged weakness, the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Thursday cut its 2004 gross domestic product forecast to 4.7% and predicted 2005 to expand just 4.0%. ("=UPDATE: Bank Of Korea Freezes Rate Despite Pessimism," ran at 0838 GMT; 2nd UPDATE to run around 1030 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: China's value-added industrial output decelerated to its slowest growth rate in 10 months in November, although some sectors targeted by the government's slowdown measures continued to show robust expansion. ("=DATA VIEW: China Nov Indus Output Slowest In 10 Months," ran at 0615 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: China's trade surplus widened to $9.9 billion in November from $7.1 billion in October, which is likely to provide more ammunition to U.S. critics of China's exchange rate regime. The result is also wider than a $4.9 billion surplus in November 2003, and the surge in exports should help to cushion effects of the government's crackdown on investment. (First take to run shortly)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japan's core machinery orders fell for the second straight month in October, government data showed Thursday, in yet another sign the nation's export-led economic recovery is faltering. ("=UPDATE: Weak Japan Machinery Orders Show Econ Faltering," ran at 0903 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: A surprise increase in employment sent Australia's jobless rate to a new 27-year low in November, reinvigorating expectations the Reserve Bank of Australia will need to raise interest rates next year and destabilizing minority calls for rate cuts. ("=UPDATE: Australia 27-Yr Low Jobless Boosts RBA Rise Case," ran at 0432 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =JAPAN CREDIT: Approval last month by Japan's parliament of changes in trust business law will make it easier to securitize intellectual property rights in Japan, spurring further development of the country's securitization market. ("=JAPAN CREDIT: Securitizing Intellectual Property Easier," ran at 0110 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ASIA: The Australian dollar has stumbled in its race to a fresh eight year high, ranking as one of the worst global performers over the past two weeks due to weaker economic readings and commodity price weakness. ("=\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ASIA: Australian Dlr Fizzles But Support Maybe Near," ran at 0605 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: A senior German economic official questioned Thurday whether joint \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by both \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be effective, adding that the current sitation with the euro around all-time highs would "normally" call for concerted action by the ECB, U.S. and Japan. (INTERVIEW to run soon)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: The New Zealand \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  held the official short-term interest rate steady at 6.5% Thursday, and surprised the market by saying it might raise rates again if inflationary pressures continue to build. ("=2nd UPDATE: RBNZ Holds Interest Rates; Says Could Rise," ran at 0352 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Signs that some major central banks are reassessing their reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  shares are a "warning" to the U.S. that it must take steps to maintain the dollar's status as a key \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a former senior International Monetary Fund official said. ("=INTERVIEW:Shifting \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Reserves Warning To US-Ex IMF Aide," ran at 0721 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japan's next big challenge, once deflation has ended, is managing increases in interest rates, which are a big risk for the government in funding its large deficit, Standard & Poor's sovereign analyst for Japan said Thursday. ("=INTERVIEW: S&P Analyst: Rate Rises A Challenge For Japan," ran at 0633 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 EQUITIES  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Foreign investors may have to wait until after Hong Kong's Housing Authority lists its real estate investment trust to get a piece of the action. Sources close to the Link REIT said the authority is under increasing pressure to allocate all of the available units of the initial public offering to Hong Kong investors, including the city's pension funds. ("Foreign Investors May Not Get Slice Of HK Link REIT IPO," ran at 0547 GMT; UPDATE to run after 0900 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: Industrial & Commercial Bank of China, China's largest state-owned bank, is looking to a possible government recapitalization funded from the country's foreign reserves, a senior bank executive said Thursday. That capital infusion along with a planned domestic bond issue are expected to set the stage for a public share offering both domestically and overseas, ICBC Executive Vice President Zhang Furong told Dow Jones Newswires. ("=EXCLUSIVE: China ICBC Exec: Govt Recap Likely From \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," first take ran at 0631 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 PERTH: Trying to derail Xstrata Plc.'s hostile takeover bid, WMC Resources Ltd. Thursday said it will return up to A$1 billion to shareholders and tipped a record A$1.3 billion 2004 profit due to soaring metal prices, tax credits and insurance payouts. ("=UPDATE: Australia's WMC Ups Stakes In Xstrata Battle," ran at 0828 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Dubai Ports International said Thursday it has signed an agreement to acquire CSX Corp.'s international container terminal business for US$1.15 billion. ("Dubai Ports Says It Will Buy US CSX's Intl Terminal Ops," first take ran at 0225 GMT; UPDATE to run shortly)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: In another sign of reorganization in Japan's banking and securities industries, Mizuho Financial Group Inc. said Thursday it is considering taking a stake in Nikko Cordial Corp. ("=UPDATE: Mizuho FG Mulls Taking Stake In Nikko Cordial," ran at 0756 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Air China Ltd., the country's largest airline, will likely make a strong debut on the Hong Kong Stock Exchange's main board on Dec. 15, boosted by robust institutional demand, said analysts. ("=UPDATE: Air China IPO Likely To Make Strong Debut Dec 15," ran at 0447 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: China's second-largest telecommunications equipment maker ZTE Corp. Thursday staged a strong debut on the Hong Kong stock exchange's main board, with its shares rising as much as 10.5% in early trade. ("China's ZTE Sharply Higher In HK Main Board Debut," ran at 0219 GMT; UPDATE to run by around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Suntec Real Estate Investment Trust and Yellow Pages (Singapore) Ltd. both closed higher on their debuts Thursday, underscoring investors' growing preference for stocks that offer strong dividend income. The Suntec and Yellow Pages IPOs are the first and third-largest IPOs, respectively, in Singapore this year. (Story to run by around 1015 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: PalmSource Inc. agreed to acquire mobile phone software company China MobileSoft Ltd. for about 1.57 million shares, facilitating the development of a Linux-based version of Palm OS. (Story to run by around 1115 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japanese game maker Nintendo Corp. President Satoru Iwata said Thursday it will boost output of its new portable game player Nintendo DS to 1.5 million units a month from April. ("Nintendo Plans To Boost DS Monthly Output After April," first take ran at 0535 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japanese auto makers have posted record profits and increased their global market share, but their very success has caused some companies to hit a speed bump - they haven't been able to secure enough materials to build all the cars sought by customers. ("=Japan Auto Makers Grapple With Steel Supply Constraints," ran at 0635 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Canon Inc. plans to spend about Y5 billion to build a laser printer factory in Vietnam, which it expects will come on line in 2006, a spokesman for the Japanese company said Thursday. ("Canon: To Invest Y5B In Vietnam Laser Printer Plant," first take ran at 2340 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: Fonterra Cooperative Group Ltd. Chief Executive Andrew Ferrier said Thursday that he wouldn't rule out raising the New Zealand company's A$5.45-a-share bid for Australian dairy target National Foods Ltd. ("NZ Fonterra CEO Says Won't Rule Out Higher Natl Foods Bid," ran at 0004 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Tom Group to announce a strategic investment. (Story expected after 0900 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TAIPEI: Taiwan on Thursday sold debt-laden Chung Shing Bank to Union Bank of Taiwan, the surprise winner in what had been expected to be a fierce bidding competition, for a surprisingly low price ("=UPDATE:Taiwan Sells Chung Shing Bank To Surprise Winner," moving shortly)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Chinese companies will again dominate Asia's initial-public-offering scene in 2005, and are set to sell as much as US$40 billion worth of shares to global investors seeking a stake in one of the world's fastest-growing economies. Chinese banks will be at the forefront of the fund-raising, with four banks alone poised to raise a third of the total amount expected from China in 2005 - swamping issuance from other Asian markets, investment bankers say. ("=OUTLOOK 05:China IPOs Take Centrestage; Spotlight On Bks," ran at 0822 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: China's top securities regulator issued guidelines Thursday that will allow shareholders of listed firms to use electronic or other means to vote in shareholder meetings. ("China Allows Electronic, Proxy Vote System For Hldr Mtgs," ran at 0459 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TAIPEI: Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing Co., the world's largest contract chip maker, said Thursday its monthly revenue in November rose 13.4% on year, but fell 8.6% from the previous month. ("Taiwan Semicon Mfg Nov Rev NT$20.99 Bln Vs NT$18.51 Bln," ran at 0547 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: Micron Technology Inc. expects to spend as much as $200 million to build in China a factory to package, test and assemble semiconductors as a precursor to more manufacturing investments in the country, the company's chief financial officer said Thursday. (INTERVIEW to run by around 1100 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Deloitte Touche Tohmatsu International is set to expand its \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in China and India over the next six years, in line with the U.S. full-service audit firm's confidence in the long-term economic growth prospects of both countries. ("=INTERVIEW: Deloitte Plans Expansion In China, India," ran at 0814 GMT) [ 09-12-04 0937GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Honda Motor Co. has taken legal action against two Chinese automakers over design rights concerning its sport utility vehicle and is considering taking similar steps against nine others, a Honda spokeswoman said Thursday. ("Honda Files Suit Vs 2 China Cos, Mulls Steps Vs 9 Others," ran at 0731 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Sanyo Electric CO. said Thursday it will spend more than Y10 billion over the next two years to prepare for mass production of optical pickups for next-generation DVD players and recorders. ("Sanyo To Spend Y10B For Mass Output Of Optical Pickups," ran at 0146 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: Continued expectations of a rise in the value of China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  should give a modest boost to yuan-denominated shares in 2005, analysts say. ("=OUTLOOK 05:Prospect Of Stronger Yuan May Buoy China Shrs," ran at 0334 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: SK Group companies led by SK Networks Co., and U.S.-based MetLife Inc. are close to signing a definitive agreement for the sale of unlisted SK Life Insurance Co., said an executive at SK Group. ("=INTERVIEW:SK Grp Cos, MetLife Near Pact On SK Life Sale," ran at 0618 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI: The power struggle between the brothers atop the Reliance group - India's largest privately held conglomerate - may be unsettling to the growing number of investors in India Inc. But the fight probably won't derail the textiles-to-telecommunications company's underlying fundamentals, which are likely to thrive along with the Indian economy, people close to the situation say. ("=Asian Stock Focus/India:Reliance Stk Hurt By Family Feud," ran at 0549 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: South African retailer Pick 'n Pay Stores Ltd. is keeping a close eye on moves to consolidate Australia's retail sector, but currently doesn't have any "aggressive" intentions, says Deputy Chairman David Robins. ("=INTERVIEW: South Africa's Pick 'N Pay Monitors Australia," ran at 0222 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MELBOURNE: Australian Competition and Consumer Commission chairman Graeme Samuel said Thursday that he has no comment on packaging group Amcor Ltd. but expects more prosecutions relating to alleged cartel activities. ("ACCC Wants Heavy Jail Penalties For Cartel Offenders," first take ran at 0142 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: The amount of spending on gaming in New Zealand continued to grow in the fiscal year to June 30 by rising 9% over the previous year, but tighter regulations have pegged back the growth rate, the Department of Internal Affairs said Thursday. ("Spending In NZ Gaming +9% In FY But Rate Of Growth Slows" ran at 2257 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: China's Chongqing Changan Automobile Co. and the parent of light truck and van producer Jiangling Motors Co. plan to increase their investment in a recently formed holding company joint venture to CNY1 billion from CNY100 million. ("China Changan Auto, Jiangling To Raise Investment In JV," ran at 0453 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: Invitrogen Corp., a provider of life science research and production tools, expects fast growth of China's biotechnology industry to boost sales in the Asia-Pacific region, a senior company official said. ("=INTERVIEW: Invitrogen Sees Growth In China Biotech Indus," ran at 0517 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 ENERGY/COMMODITIES  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: China will have added 51,000 megawatts of new electricity generating capacity by the end of the year, according to the latest estimates by economic planners, far more than it had originally planned. ("China To Overshoot Power Plant Construction Target In '04," ran at 0337 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK: Thai Oil PCL, the country's largest oil refiner, Thursday said its board has approved a plan to buy a 24% stake in Thai Oil's unit, Independent Power Ltd., from Unocal Asia Pacific Ventures for $12.75 million. ("Thai Oil To Buy 24% Stake In Independent Pwr From Unocal," ran at 0500 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 NEW DELHI: India's farm good exports may fall sharply in 2005, while imports of key items such as edible oils and pulses look set to rise as lower output in 2004 will reduce the availability of most farm goods next year. ("=OUTLOOK 05:India Farm Exports Look Bleak;Imports To Rise," ran at 0410 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japan's Teikoku Oil Co. said Thursday its board has agreed to buy a 63.7% stake in state-owned Japan National Oil Corp.'s unit, Teikoku Oil (Sanvi Guere) Co., which mainly develops crude oil in Venezuela. ("Japan Teikoku Oil To Buy 63.7% In JNOC Venezuela Oil Proj," ran at 0557 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: China's coal output in the first 11 months rose 17.6% to 1.76 billion metric tons, the latest official data show, already surpassing 2003's all-time high of 1.7 billion tons. ("China Jan-Nov Coal Output Rises 17.6% To 1.76 Bln Tons," ran at 0352 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MANILA: PNOC-Energy Development Corp., or PNOC-EDC, a Philippine state company that develops and operates various geothermal power plants, Thursday said it plans to sell 9 billion shares next year to raise funds for new power projects and to support the government's budget. ("Philippine PNOC-Energy Devt Plans Pvt Placement, IPO '05," first take ran at 0630 GMT) [ 09-12-04 0937GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 62550  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020041209e0c9000ma\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
