\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar rises as market eyes yields, sheds risk.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Andrea Ricci  \par \uc2 649 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Dezember 2004\par 19:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Dec 9 (Reuters) - The dollar rose on Thursday as traders, short of dollars after relentlessly selling them for weeks, looked to the growing yield advantage of U.S. assets as a reason to buy back the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  before the end of the year.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts said, however, that the dollar's long-term downtrend was still intact.  \par \par \uc2 The central banks of Britain, New Zealand and Sweden on Thursday joined those of Canada and Australia in deciding to hold interest rates steady rather than raise them.  \par \par \uc2 As U.S. rates rise while those of other countries hold steady or fall, it becomes less expensive or more rewarding for investors to hold dollar-denominated assets such as Treasuries. The Federal Reserve is widely expected to raise its key rate a quarter percentage point next Tuesday to 2.25 percent.  \par \par \uc2 While the global rate outlook remains murky, the Fed is the only major \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  expected to raise rates soon. New Zealand made clear more tightening could be in the pipeline, but others this week were silent or decidedly more dovish.  \par \par \uc2 "Some of the interest rates are starting to flip around, with the U.S. hiking while others might pause. That is changing the dollar's scenario a tad, but right now (the dollar's rally) is still being called a correction and not causing any damage to the major trend of dollar weakness," said Grant Wilson, vice president at Mellon Bank in Pittsburgh.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar has fallen sharply in recent months as investors fret over the funding of the huge U.S. current account gap at a time when central banks are widely expected to diversify reserves out of dollars. Most analysts believe the dollar's long-term downtrend remains intact.  \par \par \uc2 Richard Franulovich, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Westpac Banking Corp in New York, said the dollar's gains over the past two days reflected "unwinding going into an illiquid Christmas season, which tends to discourage taking risk and has people unwinding risk."  \par \par \uc2 By early afternoon in New York, the euro was down 0.5 percent at $1.3270.  \par \par \uc2 Sterling was down 0.8 percent at $1.9173.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar rose 0.5 percent to 1.1549 Swiss francs.  \par \par \uc2 Against the Canadian dollar, the dollar rose 0.8 percent to C$1.2240, its third straight day of hefty gains.  \par \par \uc2 WEAKNESS IN JAPAN  \par \par \uc2 Against the yen, the dollar rose 0.8 percent to 104.94 yen, helped by news that machinery orders in Japan fell more than expected.  \par \par \uc2 Mellon Bank's Wilson said evidence of a slowing in Japan's recovery would raise the likelihood that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to blunt yen strength to help its export sector. A strong yen makes Japanese goods more expensive.  \par \par \uc2 "The weak data over there means they need to sell more stuff to us and that there will be less tolerance for a strong yen and that the Bank of Japan will have an interest in keeping dollar/yen over 102," said Wilson.  \par \par \uc2 A number of European financial leaders on Thursday reiterated their concern about the risks to their countries' economies from the rising euro. Italian Prime Minister Silvio Berlusconi, a close ally of U.S. President George W. Bush, said he would speak to Bush about the sharp rise in the euro.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market showed little reaction to news initial U.S. jobless claims rose to 357,000 in the latest week from 349,000. Economists had expected a fall to 335,000.  \par \par \uc2 A rise in the price of imported goods in November as non-petroleum prices hit a 10-month high was seen as underscoring the view that the Fed would raise interest rates to stem inflation. U.S. import prices rose 0.2 percent overall, while non-petroleum import prices were up 0.7 percent.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041209e0c9008vi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
