\par\par \b \uc2 Japan Auto Mfrs Assoc: \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Rates Ought To Be Set By Mkt  \b0\par\par\uc2 394 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Dezember 2004\par 09:59\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates ought to be left up to market forces, though stability in the yen versus other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  is desirable, a top official at the Japan Automobile Manufacturers' Association said Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 "Exchange rates should be decided by the market. What we call for is stability of the exchange rate itself," said Yoshiyasu Nao, vice chairman and president of the industry body.  \par \par \uc2 "The most important thing is stability of the level itself," he told Dow Jones Newswires in an interview. [ 08-12-04 0900GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 The comments come in the wake of a surge in the yen to a nearly five-year high against the dollar last week. The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has recovered some of its losses, partly on the back of warnings by Japanese monetary authorities who are worried a strong yen could hurt the already-slowing economy.  \par \par \uc2 Nao, who began his career 30 years ago at the Ministry of International Trade and Industry - now called the Ministry of Economy, Trade and Industry, declined to comment on specific levels of the yen. He also emphasized that his comments weren't aimed at influencing movements in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.  \par \par \uc2 Authority over Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy lies with the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Officials at the MOF have been at pains to talk down the yen recently, though \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders say there haven't been any signs of actual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's automakers are more resilient to a stronger yen than in the past, as they have steadily shifted manufacturing \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  to foreign countries, where they also sell cars, Nao said.  \par \par \uc2 "So the risk (posed by) changes in the exchange rate has been reduced dramatically over the past 10 to 20 years," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Asked if he expected any complaints from Washington as Japanese automakers such as Toyota Motor Corp. (7203.TO) grab a bigger slice of the U.S. market, Nao said he was confident a trade row with Japan's major trading partner wasn't in the offing.  \par \par \uc2 "I don't anticipate any trade friction with the United States because many (Japanese) cars sold in the U.S. market are made in America," he said.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Arran Scott, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; arran.scott@dowjones.com   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Hugh Lawson [ 08-12-04 0924GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020041208e0c8000g4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
